const LIKELY_WHY = {
  "cq1-1": "This asks what is true when the rate depends on the concentration. Two are correct: the half-life is independent of concentration, and the rate equation is d C d t equals minus k C. A rate that depends on concentration is first order: the rate equals k times C, so the rate changes with the concentration. The first-order half-life is zero point six nine three divided by k, and neither term contains a concentration, so the time to halve is the same from any level. A half-life that depends on concentration belongs to zero order, where it is C zero divided by twice k. The equation d C d t equals minus k has no C, so it is the zero-order rate law. She said, if the rate depends on concentration, remember our half-life is independent, our half-life is zero point six nine three over K, doesn't change.",
  "cq1-2": "This asks what holds for a zero-order process. The answer: the elimination rate is independent of the amount of drug in the body. Zero order means d C d t equals minus k, with no amount or concentration term, so the same quantity leaves in each unit of time however much drug is present. The tempting choice, the rate at its highest when the amount is at its highest, describes first order, where the rate is k times the amount. She said of a first-order process, The rate is highest at the top of the curve when the concentration is highest. A rate that increases as a function of time fits neither order: in zero order the rate is constant, and in first order it falls as the amount falls.",
  "fr-1": "This asks for the property of a first-order process. The answer is a constant half-life. The first-order half-life is zero point six nine three divided by k, and k is a constant, so the half-life does not depend on how much drug is present. A constant rate of elimination is the zero-order property: in first order, d C d t equals minus k C, so the rate keeps changing as the concentration falls, and d C d t equals minus k is the zero-order rate law. Units of concentration or amount per time belong to zero order; a first-order k has units of reciprocal time. She said, So the correct answer here. Was a constant half-life zero point six nine three over K gives us our half-life, right? Rate of elimination is constantly changing. DCDT is equal to minus KC. And appropriate units of k are reciprocal time.",
  "fr-3": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is about one hour. A half-life is the time between a level and half of it. Half of one hundred is roughly fifty, and the points near those levels are ninety-three micrograms per milliliter at one hour and forty-four micrograms per milliliter at two hours, one hour apart. Each hour the level falls to about half, since forty-four divided by twenty-one is also about two, and a constant fractional fall is first order, so the half-life is the same all along the curve. From the two points, k is the natural log of their ratio divided by the one hour between them, zero point seven four eight per hour, and the half-life is zero point six nine three divided by that k, zero point nine three hours. Both read the same half-life to one significant figure. She said, Half-Life is the time that it takes to go to half, so half of one hundred is roughly fifty, so ninety-three down to forty-four. For this example, one hour.",
  "m1-ord-c07": "This asks which order describes the fall in the amount of drug A. The answer is zero order. From twenty to forty minutes the amount falls from eighty-nine point zero to seventy-three point zero milligrams, and from forty to sixty minutes from seventy-three point zero to fifty-seven point zero milligrams: sixteen point zero milligrams lost in each twenty minutes. Equal quantities lost in equal intervals, constant differences rather than ratios, is the zero-order signature. Choosing first order reads a continuous fall as first order, but first order needs a constant ratio, and over those twenty-minute spans the ratio climbs from one point two two to one point two eight. Choosing neither order treats the unequal spacing of the time points as a reason the data cannot be classified, but unequal spacing adds arithmetic, not ambiguity. She said, Zero-order, the drug is decreasing at a constant amount per unit time.",
  "m1-ord-n01": "This asks for the first-order rate constant k, in per hour. The answer is zero point zero eight six six per hour. The order is settled first: over equal twelve-hour spans the ratio is constant, two point eight three from twelve to twenty-four hours and again from twenty-four to thirty-six hours, so a fixed fraction is lost per unit time and the process is first order. Subtracting the integrated equation at two times cancels C zero, so k is the natural log of the earlier concentration over the later one, divided by the time between them. Her key uses two hundred eight point one milligrams per liter at six hours and forty-three point eight milligrams per liter at twenty-four hours, eighteen hours apart. The log of that ratio, one point five five eight three eight, divided by eighteen hours gives zero point zero eight six five eight per hour, reported to four decimal places as zero point zero eight six six per hour.",
  "ws1-3-1a": "This asks how to classify the decrease in the amount of drug A. The answer is zero order. The drug is decreasing at a constant amount per unit time: sixteen point zero milligrams in each twenty minutes from twenty to sixty minutes. Equal amounts lost in equal times is zero order, a straight line of amount against time. First order would lose the same fraction in equal times, but over the twenty-minute intervals the ratio grows: eighty-nine point zero divided by seventy-three point zero is one point two one nine, and seventy-three point zero divided by fifty-seven point zero is one point two eight one. The fraction lost is not constant, so first order is wrong. Neither order fits the data is wrong too: the uneven spacing of the times does not prevent a decision, because over equal twenty-minute intervals the amount falls by the same sixteen point zero milligrams.",
  "ws1-3-1b": "This asks for the zero-order rate constant, in milligrams per minute. The answer is zero point eight milligrams per minute. Part a settled zero order, so the amount follows the straight line A equals A zero minus k t, and k is the slope with the sign reversed. Her key takes the twenty and sixty minute points: eighty-nine point zero milligrams at twenty minutes and fifty-seven point zero milligrams at sixty minutes. Thirty-two milligrams are lost in forty minutes, so k is thirty-two milligrams divided by forty minutes, zero point eight milligrams per minute. The unit is an amount per time because the order is zero: a fixed zero point eight milligrams of drug A is lost each minute. Any two rows of the table give a slope between zero point seven five and zero point eight milligrams per minute.",
  "x3-a-q1-zero": "This asks what is true when the rate is independent of the concentration. Two are correct: the half-life depends on the concentration of the drug present, and the rate equation is d C d t equals minus k. That equation has no concentration term, so the same amount is lost per unit time whatever is present: zero order. The zero-order half-life is C zero divided by twice k, so a higher starting level takes longer to halve. Picking the option that the half-life is independent of concentration carries the first-order half-life across, and d C d t equals minus k C is first order, the opposite of the process in the stem. She said, Our stem says independent of concentration, so you should have picked for this one, A and D. In her lettering, those are the half-life that depends on concentration and the rate equation without C.",
  "x3-a-tf-rate": "This asks whether the rate of a first-order process is independent of the concentration. The answer is False. The first-order rate depends on the concentration present: d C d t equals minus k C. On the curve the slope is steepest at the start, where there is most drug, and flattens as the drug is eliminated. Picking True reads independent as a first-order property. A rate independent of concentration is zero order, d C d t equals minus k. What is independent of concentration in first order is the half-life, zero point six nine three divided by k. She said, If we're thinking about the rate being the slope, higher, steeper slope up here where we've got more drug in the body, so the rate depends on the concentration that's there.",
  "cq1-3a": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is five point zero hours. An I V bolus following C equals C zero e to the minus k t means k comes from two points, and the half-life is zero point six nine three divided by k. The weight and the dose are not needed. The first and last points span the most time: one hundred twelve micrograms per milliliter at zero point five hours and thirteen point one micrograms per milliliter at sixteen hours. k is the natural log of one hundred twelve divided by thirteen point one, which is two point one four five nine, divided by fifteen point five hours, giving zero point one three eight four four per hour. The half-life is zero point six nine three divided by that k, five point zero zero six hours, reported as five point zero hours. She accepted four point nine seven. She said, if you give me four point nine seven, now, if you give me four point one nine, something's funky.",
  "cq1-3b": "This asks for the apparent volume of distribution, V D, in liters. The answer is about eighteen point eight liters. V D equals D zero divided by C zero. One hundred sixty-five pounds is seventy-five kilograms, so the thirty milligram per kilogram dose is two thousand two hundred fifty milligrams. The first sample was drawn at zero point five hours, not at time zero, so it is run back with k from part a: one hundred twelve milligrams per liter times e raised to the product of zero point one three eight four four per hour and zero point five hours, which is one hundred twelve times one point zero seven one seven, gives a C zero of one hundred twenty point zero three milligrams per liter. Two thousand two hundred fifty milligrams divided by one hundred twenty point zero three milligrams per liter is eighteen point seven five liters, about eighteen point eight liters. She said, You can't use D zero and the concentration thirty minutes after the dose was given. So you gotta go back up and find C zero.",
  "cq2-2": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is four point six two hours. A, B, alpha and beta for an I V bolus dose name the two-compartment model. The elimination half-life belongs to the terminal phase, whose slope is beta, so the half-life equals zero point six nine three divided by beta. The distribution constant alpha, three point five nine per hour, describes the early fall that ends once the tissues have filled. A and B are intercepts, not rates, and the half-life does not depend on the two hundred fifty milligram dose, so none of them enters. Zero point six nine three divided by zero point one five per hour is four point six two hours, because a pure number over reciprocal hours leaves hours. Rounding to the nearest hundredth changes nothing. The elimination half-life uses the smaller exponent, beta, not the larger, alpha.",
  "cq2-3": "This asks for the plasma concentration C p four hours after the dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is one point seven two milligrams per liter. A, B, alpha and beta name the two-compartment model, so C p equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t, with A paired with alpha and B with beta. The distribution term is ten point six eight milligrams per liter times e to the minus fourteen point two, since alpha times four hours is fourteen point two, and that term is zero point zero zero zero zero because alpha is large. The terminal term is three point one three milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point six, since beta times four hours is zero point six. That term is three point one three times zero point five four eight eight, giving one point seven one seven eight milligrams per liter, rounded to one point seven two milligrams per liter. Swapping the intercepts between the rate constants is the usual error.",
  "fr-2": "This asks for the apparent volume of distribution. The answer is ten liters. For an I V bolus, C p equals C zero e to the minus k t, so the forty milligrams per liter in front of the exponential is C zero. V D equals D zero divided by C zero: four hundred milligrams divided by forty milligrams per liter, ten liters. Picking forty liters reads the forty as a volume, though it is a concentration in milligrams per liter. Picking twenty liters divides the dose by half the coefficient. The zero point nine is k, in per hour, and takes no part in the volume. She said, And most of you guys thought ten liters. That is the correct answer. So we're taking this four hundred milligrams and dividing it by the forty milligrams per liter, OK?",
  "hw2-2a": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is four point five zero hours. The stem gives a two-exponential equation after I V bolus administration, so the two-compartment model applies. Alpha, the larger exponent, describes distribution. Beta, the smaller exponent, zero point one five four per hour, describes elimination. So the elimination half-life is zero point six nine three divided by beta: zero point six nine three divided by zero point one five four per hour, four point five zero hours. The intercepts, eight point seven two and five point four zero micrograms per milliliter, are not rates, and the three hundred fifty milligram dose does not change a half-life, so they are set aside. The slip is using the larger exponent, alpha, which is distribution and not the half-life asked for. The alpha term has a half-life well under an hour.",
  "m2-n-999a": "This asks for the time, in hours, for ninety-nine point nine percent of the drug to be eliminated. The answer is seven point six hours. A first-order process removes proportions, not amounts, so eliminating a fixed percentage takes a fixed number of half-lives, whatever the dose. Ninety-nine point nine percent eliminated leaves zero point one percent, one part in one thousand, which is reached after ten half-lives, because each half-life leaves half of what was there. The time is ten times the half-life: ten times zero point seven six hours, seven point six hours. The logarithmic route agrees: the natural log of one thousand divided by a k of zero point nine one per hour is seven point five nine one hours. She said, First order processes, it is gonna take ten half-lives for ninety-nine point nine percent of the drug to be eliminated from the body.",
  "m2-n-cl1": "This asks for the total body clearance, Cl T, in liters per hour. The answer is eleven point seven liters per hour. Total body clearance from a volume of distribution and an elimination rate constant is their product: Cl T equals k times V D. This relationship is not on the equation sheet, so it has to be recalled. k is the fraction of the body content that leaves per hour, and V D is how large the body content appears to be, so their product is the volume cleared of drug per hour. Twelve point eight five liters times zero point nine one per hour is eleven point six nine three five liters per hour, reported as eleven point seven liters per hour. Her key reads, Cl T equals twelve point eight five liters times zero point nine one per hour equals eleven point seven liters per hour.",
  "m2-n-k1": "This asks for the elimination rate constant k, in per hour. The answer is zero point nine one zero zero per hour. After an I V bolus, a first-order decline is a straight line of natural log of concentration against time with slope minus k, so k is the natural log of the earlier concentration over the later one, divided by the time between them. Her key takes one point five nine milligrams per liter at one hour and zero point six four milligrams per liter at two hours. Their ratio is two point four eight four four, and its natural log, zero point nine one zero zero two, divided by one hour is reported to four decimal places as zero point nine one zero zero per hour. The dose and the weight are not needed. Her key reads, k equals minus the slope, equals minus the quantity natural log of zero point six four minus natural log of one point five nine, divided by the quantity two minus one, hours, equals zero point nine one zero zero per hour.",
  "m2-n-t1": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is zero point seven six hours. Half-life and the elimination rate constant are two readings of the same first-order decline, joined by the natural log of two. Setting the concentration to half its starting value in C p equals C p zero e to the minus k t leaves zero point six nine three divided by k. This relationship is not on the equation sheet, so it has to be recalled. Zero point six nine three divided by zero point nine one per hour is zero point seven six one five hours, which her key prints as zero point seven six hours. A half-life is always reported in units of time, never in reciprocal time. Her key reads, half-life equals zero point six nine three over zero point nine one per hour, equals zero point seven six hours.",
  "m2-n-tb1": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is three point five hours. A, B, alpha and beta for an I V bolus name the two-compartment model. The smaller rate constant, beta, zero point one nine eight per hour, is elimination, and an unqualified half-life in a two-compartment problem means the beta half-life. Zero point six nine three divided by zero point one nine eight per hour is three point five zero zero zero hours, reported as three point five hours. The larger exponent, alpha, three point one four per hour, belongs to distribution and is not used, and neither are the intercepts or the three hundred milligram dose. Her key reads, half-life beta equals zero point six nine three divided by zero point one nine eight per hour equals three point five hours.",
  "m2-n-vd1": "This asks for the apparent volume of distribution, V D, in liters. The answer is twelve point eight five liters. V D is the amount in the body divided by the concentration at the same instant. Immediately after a bolus the amount in the body is the whole dose, so with the initial plasma concentration, V D equals D zero divided by C p zero. Fifty milligrams divided by three point eight nine milligrams per liter is twelve point eight five three liters, reported as twelve point eight five liters. The seventy kilogram weight is given and not used. Dividing the full dose by a later measured concentration would pair an amount from one time with a concentration from another. Her key reads, V D equals fifty milligrams divided by three point eight nine milligrams per liter equals twelve point eight five liters.",
  "ws2-3-1c": "This asks for the total body clearance, Cl T, in liters per hour. The answer is two point nine five liters per hour. Cl T equals k times V D, and that line needs k and an absolute volume. First the weight: one hundred eighty-seven pounds divided by two point two pounds per kilogram is eighty-five kilograms. Twenty percent of body weight is zero point two liters per kilogram, and times eighty-five kilograms that is seventeen liters. Next, k equals zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. Then Cl T equals seventeen liters times zero point one seven three three per hour, two point nine five liters per hour, the volume of plasma cleared of drug each hour. The fifteen milligram per kilogram dose is not needed, because clearance does not depend on the dose.",
  "ws2-3-2c": "This asks for the plasma concentration C p four hours after the dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is three point eight six milligrams per liter. A, B, alpha and beta name the two-compartment model, so C p equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t, each intercept falling with its own rate constant. At four hours the alpha exponent is twelve point five six, so ten point two one milligrams per liter times e to the minus twelve point five six is zero point zero zero zero zero four milligrams per liter, almost nothing. The beta exponent is zero point seven nine two, and e to the minus zero point seven nine two is zero point four five two nine, so eight point five three milligrams per liter times zero point four five two nine gives three point eight six milligrams per liter. By four hours distribution is over and only the beta term remains. The dose is not needed.",
  "x3-a-2cpt-c4": "This asks for the plasma concentration C p four hours after the dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is three point three seven milligrams per liter. A, B, alpha and beta name the two-compartment model, so C p equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t. The A term is eight point four five milligrams per liter times e to the minus eight point seven two, which is zero point zero zero one four milligrams per liter, all but vanished because alpha is large. The B term is five point three two milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point four five six, which is five point three two times zero point six three three eight, giving three point three seven two milligrams per liter. The terms add to three point three seven three milligrams per liter, rounded to three point three seven milligrams per liter; her three point four milligrams per liter is the same to one decimal place. She said, Concentration, four hours after administration of the dose, three point four milligrams per liter.",
  "x3-a-dose2": "This asks what a twofold increase in the I V bolus dose changes for a first-order drug. Two are correct: an increased initial plasma concentration, and an increased A U C. C zero equals D zero divided by V D with V D fixed, so twice the dose gives twice C zero. A U C equals D zero divided by clearance, and clearance does not change with the dose, so A U C doubles too. An increased half-life reads a larger amount as taking longer to halve, but zero point six nine three divided by k has no dose in it. An increased clearance confuses clearance with the rate of elimination: the milligrams removed per hour double, while the volume cleared per hour does not. She said, So, remember the other pieces here for first-order processes, our clearance, our half-life, and our volume of distribution. These things are going to be constant.",
  "x3-a-ivb-t": "This asks for the elimination half-life, in hours. The answer is eight point one four hours. The dose is an amount and the six-hour level is a concentration, so the level is converted to an amount first. V D is ten percent of eighty kilograms, eight liters, taking one kilogram as one liter, and eight liters times fifteen milligrams per liter is one hundred twenty milligrams at six hours. So k is the natural log of two hundred milligrams divided by one hundred twenty milligrams, which is zero point five one zero eight, divided by six hours: zero point zero eight five one four per hour. The half-life is zero point six nine three divided by that k, eight point one four hours. With k rounded to zero point zero eight five per hour the division gives eight point one five hours, her figure. She said, OK, and then the Half-Life, what's the Half-Life? Eight point one five hours.",
  "cq2-4": "This asks why a loading dose is used. The answer: to immediately obtain a therapeutic plasma concentration. The time to steady state is set by the half-life alone: an infusion takes three to five half-lives to get there, whatever the rate. A loading dose, D L, equals C s s times V D, the steady-state concentration times the apparent volume of distribution. It puts the steady-state amount into the body at once, so the therapeutic level is there from the start and the infusion then holds it. Choosing to reduce adverse effects is wrong because a loading dose raises the concentration faster, which brings any concentration-related adverse effect forward rather than reducing it. Choosing to reduce the elimination half-life reads the half-life as something a dose can shorten, but the half-life is a property of the drug and the patient, and no dose changes k, clearance or V D.",
  "cq2-5": "This asks for the plasma concentration C p five hours after the infusion stopped, in milligrams per liter. The answer is six point eight milligrams per liter. Once the infusion stops, the drug falls by first-order elimination from the stopping concentration, so C p equals that concentration times e to the minus k t. The stem gives no k, so k equals clearance divided by V D: three point two liters per hour divided by twenty-two liters, which is zero point one four five five per hour. Then C p equals fourteen milligrams per liter times e to the minus the product of zero point one four five five per hour and five hours, which is fourteen times zero point four eight three two, giving six point seven seven milligrams per liter, rounded to six point eight milligrams per liter. The infusion rate is not needed once the stopping concentration is known.",
  "cq2-6": "This asks what follows when the infusion rate is increased. The answer: the steady-state concentration increases. C s s equals R divided by clearance. At the plateau the rate in, R, equals the rate out, clearance times C s s. Clearance is a constant for the patient, so a larger R must be balanced by a larger C s s, and doubling the rate doubles C s s. Choosing that the time to reach steady state increases reads a higher plateau as a longer climb. Choosing that the time decreases reads a faster input as a shorter wait. Both fail for the same reason: the fraction of steady state reached is one minus e to the minus k t, which has no rate term, so steady state takes three to five half-lives at any rate. Fifty percent of C s s is reached after one half-life and ninety percent at three point three half-lives.",
  "cq2-7": "This asks for the infusion rate R, in milligrams per hour. The answer is sixty milligrams per hour. At steady state the rate in equals the rate out, so C s s equals R divided by clearance, rearranged to R equals C s s times clearance. Clearance is not given, so it is built as Cl equals k times V D. First, twenty-three micrograms per milliliter is twenty-three milligrams per liter. Next, k equals zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. Clearance is zero point one seven three three per hour times fifteen liters, two point five nine nine liters per hour. Then R equals twenty-three milligrams per liter times two point five nine nine liters per hour, fifty-nine point eight milligrams per hour. Rounded to the nearest whole number, that is sixty milligrams per hour.",
  "cq2-8": "This asks for the expected steady-state concentration, C s s, in milligrams per liter. The answer is fourteen point nine milligrams per liter. A continuous I V infusion gives C s s equals R divided by clearance, with clearance built from the half-life and a weight-based V D. One hundred fifty-four pounds divided by two point two pounds per kilogram is seventy kilograms, and zero point two one liters per kilogram times seventy kilograms is fourteen point seven liters. k equals zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. Clearance is zero point one seven three three per hour times fourteen point seven liters, two point five four seven liters per hour. Thirty-eight milligrams per hour divided by two point five four seven liters per hour gives fourteen point nine two milligrams per liter, rounded to fourteen point nine milligrams per liter. The age and sex are stated and not used.",
  "fig-inf-1": "This asks what kind of input produces a curve that climbs from zero and levels off. The answer is a constant-rate intravenous infusion. Drug goes in at a fixed rate R, a zero-order input, and comes out by a first-order process, so the rate out is proportional to the concentration present. As the concentration rises, the rate out rises with it and the climb slows, until rate in equals rate out at the steady state, where C s s equals R divided by clearance. An I V bolus is wrong: the whole dose goes in at once, so the concentration is highest at time zero and only falls. A single oral dose peaks and then falls, and repeated oral doses climb with each dose and fall between them; this curve does neither. She said, Our input is zero order, constant in, first order out. When we stop the in, then it's just out.",
  "fig-inf-2": "This asks what is true of the same drug infused into the same patient at two rates. The answer: both curves reach their plateau at the same time. The infusion rate R sets only the height of the plateau: C s s equals R divided by clearance. The approach depends on k, the elimination rate constant, so both curves take the same three to five half-lives. The tempting answer, that the higher rate reaches steady state sooner, reads the steeper early climb as a faster approach; both curves have closed the same fraction of their own gap at every moment. She said, changing the rate changes our steady state concentration with me. I feel like I've said it five times, and I've said it five times because this is one of those things that I want you to take with you. so I've said it five times. I'm gonna ask it of you ten times.",
  "fr-12a": "This asks for the plasma concentration at the end of a two hour infusion. The answer is four point four five milligrams per liter. During the infusion, C p equals R divided by clearance, times the difference one minus e to the minus k t. With k of zero point two three one per hour, R divided by clearance is fifty milligrams per hour divided by the product of zero point two three one per hour and eighteen liters, which is twelve point zero two five milligrams per liter, the steady state the level climbs toward. After two hours, one minus e to the minus zero point four six two is zero point three seven zero zero, and that fraction of the steady state is four point four four nine milligrams per liter. Three point zero three milligrams per liter is twenty-five percent of steady state, an undercount, and four point eight one milligrams per liter is forty percent, an overcount. Thirty-one point five milligrams per liter is above the steady state, which an infusion never passes. She said, OK, still going? All right, still working, are we done? OK. Four point four five milligrams per liter.",
  "fr-12b": "This asks for the plasma concentration three hours after the infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is two point two milligrams per liter. Once the infusion ends no drug goes in, so only first-order elimination remains, starting from the four point four five milligrams per liter of part a: C p equals that level times e to the minus k t. k equals zero point six nine three divided by three hours, which is zero point two three one per hour. Three hours is exactly one half-life, so e to the minus zero point six nine three is zero point five zero zero, and half of four point four five milligrams per liter is two point two two five milligrams per liter, reported as two point two milligrams per liter. She said, What is the concentration three hours after cessation? Am I got an answer for me? Two point two? OK. Everybody get two point two.",
  "fr-12d": "This asks for the steady-state concentration, C s s, in milligrams per liter. The answer is about twelve milligrams per liter. At steady state the rate in equals the rate out, so C s s equals R divided by clearance, with clearance equal to k times V D. k equals zero point six nine three divided by three hours, which is zero point two three one per hour, and clearance is that times eighteen liters, four point one five eight liters per hour. Fifty milligrams per hour divided by four point one five eight liters per hour gives twelve point zero two five milligrams per liter, rounded to the nearest whole number, twelve milligrams per liter. She said, What if we were to continue this infusion? At this rate, What would the steady state concentration be? I got an answer? What do you got? Twelve point zero two. OK. If I get twelve-ish?",
  "fr-12e": "This asks for the steady-state concentration, C s s, in milligrams per liter, at one hundred milligrams per hour. The answer is twenty-four milligrams per liter. C s s equals R divided by clearance. Clearance, k times V D from the three hour half-life and the eighteen liter volume, belongs to the drug and the patient, not to the infusion rate, so it stays at four point one five eight liters per hour and only R changes. One hundred milligrams per hour divided by four point one five eight liters per hour is twenty-four point zero five milligrams per liter, rounded to twenty-four milligrams per liter. Twice the rate of part d gives twice its twelve milligrams per liter, in the same three to five half-lives. She said, If we. Infuse it at a rate of one hundred milligrams per hour, what would the steady state concentration be? Twenty-four.",
  "fr-12f": "This asks how long reaching twenty-four milligrams per liter at one hundred milligrams per hour takes, compared with reaching twelve milligrams per liter at fifty milligrams per hour. The answer is the same time. Changing the rate changes only the concentration. The climb follows one minus e to the minus k t, which holds only k, so either rate reaches steady state in three to five half-lives of three hours, nine to fifteen hours. Choosing half the time reads a faster input as a faster arrival. Choosing twice the time assumes a higher plateau needs more time to reach. The plateau doubles with the rate, but so does the speed of the climb at every moment, so the fraction of steady state reached at each time is the same. She said, So changing the rate. Just changes the concentration. It does not change the time that it takes to get to that steady-state concentration, OK?",
  "fr-7": "This asks which type of drug input the curve shows. The answer is zero-order input. Zero order means a constant rate of input, as with an I V infusion. With first-order output, the level rises until the rate out equals the rate in and then holds at the steady-state level. First-order input reads the rise as absorption from an oral dose; first-order input slows as the absorption site empties, so the curve peaks and then falls, and this curve never turns down. Instantaneous input reads the curve as a bolus, which puts the whole dose in at time zero, so the concentration starts at its highest and only falls; this curve starts at zero and rises. She said, So, I was looking for zero order. What does zero order mean? Same Same rate. Non-changing. What, what's another word for non-changing? Constant, right? So a constant rate of input.",
  "inf-c15": "The shape of a continuous infusion curve, compared with a bolus curve. The infusion curve starts at zero and rises to a plateau. No drug has been delivered when an infusion begins, so the concentration starts at zero. The concentration climbs because drug accumulates faster than it is removed. The climb flattens because the first-order output grows to meet the fixed zero-order input. A bolus puts the whole dose in instantly, so its highest concentration is its first. A straight line for the whole infusion is a tempting wrong answer. It reads the zero-order input as the behaviour of the whole system, but first-order elimination begins as soon as drug is present, and that growing removal bends the curve over into a plateau. Starting high and falling more slowly is also wrong, because nothing is in the body when the infusion starts; that answer keeps the bolus shape. She said, I V infusion, we're starting low and we're building.",
  "inf-c2": "How the input and the output of drug are described during a continuous infusion. The answer is zero-order input, first-order output. The pump delivers a fixed amount per unit time whatever is already in the body, and that defines a zero-order process. Elimination belongs to the drug and the patient, not to the route, so it remains first order. A constant fraction of what is present leaves per unit time, so the amount leaving rises as the amount in the body rises. The input is flat while the output grows, so the two converge and the curve rises to a plateau. Zero-order input with zero-order output is the tempting wrong choice. It carries the constant-rate description across from the pump to the body, and with a fixed output no plateau could exist. First-order input with first-order output applies the elimination description to the pump, but the rate set on the pump is the same at the first minute and the last. She said, Our input is zero order, constant in, first order out. When we stop the in, then it's just out.",
  "inf-c4": "The time to reach steady state during an infusion with no loading dose. The answer is three to five half-lives. The fraction of steady state reached after infusing for a time t is one minus e to the minus k t, where k is the elimination rate constant. Each half-life closes half of the remaining gap to the plateau. So the level reaches fifty percent after one half-life, seventy-five percent after two, and eighty-seven point five percent after three. Five half-lives reach about ninety-six point nine percent, close enough to treat the level as steady. The answer depends only on the half-life. Three to five hours is the tempting wrong answer; it reads half-lives as hours, and a drug with a twelve-hour half-life is far from steady state within five hours. The loading dose does not set the time either, because the stem states that none is given and the approach is governed by k alone. She said, if I ask you how long it takes to get steady state following I V infusion, the simplest answer. Is three to five half-lives.",
  "inf-c5": "The change in the time to reach steady state when the infusion rate is doubled. The time to reach steady state will not change. The plateau, C s s, equals the infusion rate R divided by the clearance. Clearance is constant for the patient, so doubling the rate doubles the plateau. The approach to the plateau follows one minus e to the minus k t, which has no rate term. That fraction depends only on k, the elimination rate constant, and so on the half-life. So the curve rises twice as high and reaches its new plateau in the same three to five half-lives. Saying the time doubles reads the plateau and its arrival time as moving together. Saying the time is halved reads a faster input as a faster arrival. Saying the steady-state concentration will not change swaps the two quantities, because C s s is proportional to R. She said, changing the rate changes our steady state concentration ... it has no impact, right? Because it is three to five half-lives.",
  "inf-n12": "The loading dose, in milligrams. The answer is five hundred twenty-eight milligrams. The stem asks for a loading dose to achieve a steady-state concentration and gives the volume of distribution, so the line is D L equals C s s times V D. The loading dose must put into the body the amount of drug that would be there at steady state. The volume of distribution converts a plasma concentration into that amount. The target, twenty-four milligrams per liter, is one factor, and the volume, twenty-two liters, is the other. No rate constant is needed, so the half-life does not appear. Twenty-four milligrams per liter times twenty-two liters is five hundred twenty-eight milligrams, because the liters cancel. The other route, the infusion rate R divided by the elimination rate constant k, gives the same number whenever the rate was chosen for the same target; C s s times V D does not depend on the rate at all. She said, Remember, we want our loading dose to look like the amount of drug that should be in the body at steady state.",
  "inf-n13": "The plasma concentration, in milligrams per liter, three hours after the infusion stops. The answer is seven point nine two milligrams per liter. The time is counted after cessation, so the line is C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t. Five hours of infusion is one half-life, so the fraction of steady state reached is zero point five. C peak is zero point five times twenty-four milligrams per liter, which is twelve milligrams per liter. The volume of distribution is not needed, because the plateau is given. Next, k is zero point six nine three divided by five hours, which is zero point one three eight six per hour. Then C p is twelve milligrams per liter times e to the minus the product of zero point one three eight six per hour and three hours. That is twelve milligrams per liter times zero point six five nine eight, or seven point nine one seven milligrams per liter, rounded to seven point nine two. The three hours is counted from cessation, not from the start. She corrects a student who answers eighteen milligrams per liter for C peak, the two-half-life figure.",
  "inf-n15": "The plasma concentration, in milligrams per liter, four hours after the infusion stops. The answer is three point nine milligrams per liter. The infusion ran three hours without a loading dose, so C peak, the level at cessation, comes first; then C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t. The five-hour half-life gives k, zero point one three eight six per hour. Times the sixteen liter volume, k gives a clearance of two point two one eight liters per hour. C peak is forty-four point three five milligrams per hour divided by two point two one eight liters per hour, and that quotient times the quantity one minus e to the minus the product of zero point one three eight six per hour and three hours. That is six point eight zero milligrams per liter, thirty-four percent of the twenty milligrams per liter plateau. Over four hours, e to the minus k t is zero point five seven four four, so C p is six point eight zero milligrams per liter times that, or three point nine milligrams per liter. Decaying from the plateau instead of the peak gives an answer nearly three times too high.",
  "inf-n16": "The time, in hours, from the start of the infusion to ninety-five percent of the steady-state concentration. The answer is thirty point three hours. The stem gives an infusion with no loading dose and a fraction of steady state, so the line is the concentration during an infusion, solved for t. Zero point nine five equals one minus e to the minus k t, so e to the minus k t equals zero point zero five. Age and weight are set aside, because the plateau cancels out of a percentage question. Taking natural logarithms, k t equals minus the natural log of zero point zero five, which is two point nine nine five seven, rounded to three. Next, k equals zero point six nine three divided by seven hours, which is zero point zero nine nine per hour. Then t equals three divided by zero point zero nine nine per hour, which is thirty point three hours. Her second route, four point three two half-lives times seven hours, gives thirty point two four hours; the small difference comes from her rounding of four point three two.",
  "inf-n19": "The loading dose, in milligrams, that puts the target level in the body at once. The answer is three hundred twenty milligrams. The stem asks for an I V loading dose to rapidly achieve a steady-state concentration, so the line is the loading dose from the target: D L equals C s s times V D. A loading dose has to put into the body the amount that the target concentration corresponds to. The target, twenty milligrams per liter, is one factor, and the volume, sixteen liters, is the other. D L equals twenty milligrams per liter times sixteen liters, which is three hundred twenty milligrams, because the liters cancel. The half-life of five hours is set aside: how fast the drug leaves does not change how much is needed to fill the volume at the start. Her key also gives the rate route. The infusion rate R divided by the elimination rate constant k, forty-four point three five milligrams per hour divided by zero point one three eight six per hour, is also three hundred twenty milligrams. The loading dose depends on the volume and the target, while the infusion rate depends on clearance.",
  "inf-n23": "The steady-state concentration, in milligrams per liter: the plateau where rate in equals rate out. The answer is thirty-six point zero six milligrams per liter, printed in her key as thirty-six point one milligrams per liter. Only the loading dose changed, alongside the same seventy-five milligrams per hour infusion, so the line is C s s equals R divided by clearance, which carries no loading-dose term. The loading doses of seven hundred fifty and five hundred milligrams are set aside. A loading dose is a single amount given once, and once the plateau is reached whatever it contributed has been eliminated. First, k equals zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. Clearance is twelve liters times zero point one seven three three per hour. C s s equals seventy-five milligrams per hour divided by that clearance, which is thirty-six point zero six milligrams per liter, unchanged. With seven hundred fifty milligrams the level starts at sixty-two point five milligrams per liter, above the plateau, so it falls to the plateau instead of climbing to it.",
  "inf-n25": "The time, in hours, to reach ninety percent of the steady-state concentration. The answer is thirteen point two nine hours, printed in her answer as thirteen point three hours. A constant-rate infusion with no loading dose and a fraction of steady state calls for the concentration during an infusion, solved for t. Setting one minus e to the minus k t equal to zero point nine zero leaves zero point one zero in the exponential, so t equals minus the natural log of zero point one zero, divided by k. The infusion rate cancels out of the fraction and never enters. Next, k is zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. The natural log of ten is two point three zero two five eight five, and dividing by zero point one seven three three per hour gives thirteen point two eight seven hours. That is three point three two half-lives, the standard count for ninety percent. Her working reads, t equals minus l n zero point one divided by zero point one seven three three per hour, equals thirteen point two nine hours.",
  "ws3-2f": "The time, in hours, after the infusion stops for the level to fall to five micrograms per milliliter. The answer is six point two hours. The line is the concentration after an infusion stops, C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t, solved for t. With an appropriate loading dose, the level is at steady state when the infusion stops, so C peak is C s s, twenty micrograms per milliliter. The half-life of three point one hours gives k equals zero point six nine three divided by three point one hours, which is zero point two two three five per hour. The weight, age, vial size, vial concentration and volume of distribution are set aside, because the decline needs only k. Then t equals the natural log of the ratio of twenty to five, divided by k. That natural log is one point three eight six, and divided by zero point two two three five per hour it gives six point two hours. Her key also counts half-lives: twenty divided by five is four, a fall to a quarter, two halvings of three point one hours, six point two hours.",
  "ws3-4e": "The time, in hours, to reach eighty percent of the steady-state concentration without a loading dose. The answer is thirteen point nine three hours. The stem says no loading dose and asks the time to a fraction of steady state, so the line is the concentration during an infusion, solved for t. Zero point eight equals one minus e to the minus k t, so e to the minus k t equals zero point two. The rate constant k comes from the half-life: zero point six nine three divided by six hours is zero point one one five five per hour. Only the fraction and k matter, so the target concentration, the therapeutic window, the twelve-hour period, the renal and metabolic clearances and the body weight are all set aside. Then t equals minus the natural log of zero point two, which is one point six zero nine, divided by zero point one one five five per hour, giving thirteen point nine three hours. That is just over two half-lives, because seventy-five percent takes two half-lives, twelve hours, and eighty-seven point five percent takes three half-lives, eighteen hours.",
  "x3-a-p3c": "The loading dose, in milligrams. The answer is one hundred fifty milligrams. The rate is known from part b, fifteen milligrams per hour, so the line is D L equals R divided by k, the steady-state amount given at once. The seven-hour half-life gives k, zero point six nine three divided by seven hours, which is zero point zero nine nine per hour. D L is fifteen milligrams per hour divided by zero point zero nine nine per hour, which is one hundred fifty-one point five two milligrams, because the hours cancel. The check is C s s times V D. Ten micrograms per milliliter is ten milligrams per liter, and twenty-three point one percent of sixty-five kilograms is fifteen point zero one five liters. Their product is one hundred fifty point one five milligrams. She rounds the computed dose to a practical one. Her working reads, D L equals fifteen milligrams per hour divided by the quantity zero point six nine three over seven hours, equals one hundred fifty-one point five two milligrams, rounded to one hundred fifty milligrams.",
  "x3-a-p3e": "The plasma concentration, in milligrams per liter, at the end of a four-hour infusion. The answer is three point three milligrams per liter. Four hours is well short of three to five half-lives, so the line is the concentration during an infusion: C p equals R divided by Cl T, times the quantity one minus e to the minus k t, with t equal to four hours. The plateau, R divided by Cl T, is fifteen milligrams per hour divided by one point four eight six liters per hour, which is ten point zero nine four milligrams per liter. The seven-hour half-life gives k, zero point zero nine nine per hour. The fraction reached, one minus e to the minus zero point three nine six, is zero point three two seven zero. Ten point zero nine four milligrams per liter times that fraction is three point three milligrams per liter, the level where any later decay starts. Her working reads, C p equals fifteen milligrams per hour over one point four eight six liters per hour, times the quantity one minus e to the minus zero point zero nine nine times four, equals three point three milligrams per liter.",
  "x3-a-p3f": "The plasma concentration, in milligrams per liter, six hours after a four-hour infusion stops. The answer is one point eight two milligrams per liter. The time is counted after cessation, so the line is the decay, C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t. Once the infusion stops only first-order elimination remains, and it starts from the end-of-infusion level, three point three milligrams per liter, not from C s s, which applies only to an infusion run to steady state. The seven-hour half-life gives k, zero point six nine three divided by seven hours, which is zero point zero nine nine per hour. Three point three milligrams per liter times e to the minus the product of zero point zero nine nine per hour and six hours is three point three milligrams per liter times zero point five five two one, or one point eight two milligrams per liter. Six hours is a little under one half-life, so a little over half remains. Her working reads, C p equals three point three milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point zero nine nine times six, equals one point eight two milligrams per liter.",
  "x3-a-p3g": "The plasma concentration, in milligrams per liter, eight hours after an infusion run to steady state is stopped. The answer is four point five three milligrams per liter. The infusion reached steady state, so the decay after cessation starts from C s s itself, ten micrograms per milliliter, the same number as ten milligrams per liter. The line is C p equals C s s times e to the minus k t, with t counted from cessation. The seven-hour half-life gives k, zero point zero nine nine per hour. Ten milligrams per liter times e to the minus the product of zero point zero nine nine per hour and eight hours is ten milligrams per liter times zero point four five two nine, or four point five three milligrams per liter. Eight hours is a little over one half-life, so a little under half remains. Starting from three point three milligrams per liter belongs to the infusion stopped after four hours; the infusion time decides the starting level. Her working reads, C p equals ten milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point zero nine nine times eight, equals four point five three milligrams per liter.",
  "x3-a-rate2": "The effects of doubling the rate of a continuous I V infusion. Two answers are correct: an increased steady-state concentration, and an increased plasma level at two hours. C s s equals R divided by clearance, and clearance is constant for the patient, so doubling R doubles C s s. In her review, fifty milligrams per hour gave about twelve milligrams per liter, and one hundred milligrams per hour about twenty-four milligrams per liter. During the infusion, C p equals R divided by clearance, times the quantity one minus e to the minus k t. That quantity holds only k and t, so doubling R doubles the level at every time, including two hours. A longer time to steady state reads a higher plateau as a longer climb, and a shorter time reads a faster input as a faster arrival; either rate takes three to five half-lives, set by k. The half-life does not change, because clearance and V D belong to the drug in the patient. She said, Changing the rate does not change the time that it takes to get to steady state. Changing the rate only changes the steady-state concentration.",
  "cq3-2": "The direction of change in the elimination half-life when total body clearance increases. The elimination half-life will decrease. The elimination rate constant k equals clearance divided by V D, and the half-life equals zero point six nine three divided by k. So the half-life equals zero point six nine three times V D, divided by clearance. With V D unchanged, a larger clearance means a shorter half-life. The half-life is the time to remove half of the drug, and clearance is how fast drug is removed, so faster removal takes less time. Choosing an increase in half-life is the tempting error, because renal dysfunction usually lowers clearance; but the stem states that clearance increased, and the half-life follows the clearance, whatever caused the change. Choosing no change treats the half-life as an independent property of the drug, when it is set by V D and clearance together.",
  "cq3-7": "Renal clearance, in liters per hour. The answer is three point one three liters per hour. After an I V bolus with unchanged drug recovered in urine, renal clearance is the amount excreted unchanged divided by the total A U C, and A U C is D zero divided by clearance. The forty-eight hour collection is sixteen half-lives, so the seven hundred thirteen milligrams is all the drug excreted unchanged. The three-hour half-life gives k, zero point two three one per hour. Clearance is zero point two three one per hour times nineteen liters, which is four point three eight nine liters per hour. A U C is one thousand milligrams divided by four point three eight nine liters per hour, which is two hundred twenty-seven point eight milligram hours per liter. Cl R is seven hundred thirteen milligrams divided by two hundred twenty-seven point eight milligram hours per liter, which is three point one three liters per hour. The same answer comes from f e, the fraction excreted unchanged, times Cl T, the total clearance: zero point seven one three times four point three eight nine liters per hour. Renal clearance sits below the total clearance.",
  "e2r-1": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, estimated with ideal body weight. The answer is ninety-one point nine milliliters per minute. The stem says to use ideal body weight, so the line is Cockcroft-Gault, female form, and the one hundred fifty-four pound actual weight is never used. Five feet eight inches is eight inches over five feet, and the female constant is forty-five point five. So ideal body weight is forty-five point five plus two point three times eight, which is sixty-three point nine kilograms. Cockcroft-Gault is the quantity one hundred forty minus age, times ideal body weight, all divided by the product of seventy-two and serum creatinine, then times zero point eight five for a female. The top is ninety-five years times sixty-three point nine kilograms, which is six thousand seventy point five. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point seven eight milligrams per deciliter, which is fifty-six point one six. The ratio is one hundred eight point zero nine, and times zero point eight five it is ninety-one point eight eight milliliters per minute, or ninety-one point nine milliliters per minute. That is below the one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute she calls normal.",
  "e2r-2": "Renal clearance, in milliliters per minute, and its mechanism. The answer is three hundred thirty-four point seven milliliters per minute, which means filtration with active secretion. The line is Cl R equals f e, the fraction excreted unchanged, times Cl T, total body clearance, then converted. The seventy-five kilogram weight turns ten milligrams per kilogram into a seven hundred fifty milligram dose, and two point three liters per kilogram into a volume of one hundred seventy-two point five liters. The five-hour half-life gives k, zero point one three eight six per hour, so Cl T, k times V D, is twenty-three point nine one liters per hour. Forty-eight hours is nine point six half-lives, so the collection is complete, and f e is six hundred thirty milligrams divided by seven hundred fifty milligrams, which is zero point eight four. Cl R is zero point eight four times twenty-three point nine one liters per hour, which is twenty point zero eight liters per hour. Converted to milliliters per minute, renal clearance is three hundred thirty-four point seven milliliters per minute. That is above the glomerular filtration rate of about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute, so filtration alone cannot explain it.",
  "e2r-2m": "The probable mechanism of renal elimination for this drug. The answer is filtration with active secretion. The renal clearance works out to three hundred thirty-four point seven milliliters per minute, well above the glomerular filtration rate of about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Filtration cannot clear more plasma than it filters, so a transporter must be carrying extra drug from the blood into the tubule. Filtration only would give a renal clearance close to one hundred twenty milliliters per minute; choosing it reads any drug found in urine as filtered. Three hundred thirty-four point seven milliliters per minute is almost three times that. Filtration with tubular reabsorption compares in the wrong direction, often after leaving the renal clearance in liters per hour, twenty point zero eight liters per hour, instead of converting to milliliters per minute. Reabsorption would pull renal clearance below one hundred twenty milliliters per minute.",
  "e2r-7": "The probable mechanism of renal excretion for a drug with a renal clearance of about three hundred milliliters per minute. The answer is filtration with active secretion. Three hundred milliliters per minute is above the glomerular filtration rate of about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Filtration cannot clear more plasma than it filters, so a transporter must be carrying extra drug from the blood into the tubule. Choosing filtration only reads any renally excreted drug as filtered, but three hundred milliliters per minute is two point five times the filtration rate. Choosing tubular reabsorption reads the comparison backwards, because reabsorption returns filtered drug to the blood and lowers renal clearance below one hundred twenty milliliters per minute.",
  "fr-4": "What a renal clearance of forty-five milliliters per minute implies. The drug is filtered and reabsorbed. Forty-five milliliters per minute is below the glomerular filtration rate of about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. The drug is filtered, and some of it returns from the tubule to the blood, so less plasma is cleared than is filtered. Choosing renal impairment reads the renal clearance of a drug as the patient's kidney function. Kidney function is estimated with creatinine clearance; a drug's renal clearance describes how the kidney handles that drug. Choosing active secretion reads the comparison the wrong way round, because secretion lifts renal clearance above one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. She said, So the drug is filtered and reabsorbed, OK? The inference is that we've got reabsorption going on.",
  "fr-5": "Whether hepatic clearance is approximated with creatinine clearance. The answer is false. Hepatic clearance is approximated by difference: total clearance minus renal clearance. Total clearance, Cl T, is k times V D, or D zero divided by A U C for an I V dose. Renal clearance, Cl R, is f e times Cl T, from the drug recovered unchanged in the urine. No simpler measure of hepatic clearance is available. Creatinine clearance, from Cockcroft-Gault with the ideal body weight, estimates the patient's renal function, the glomerular filtration rate, and says nothing about the liver. Choosing true mixes up the two clearances. She said, how do we approximate hepatic clearance? Total minus renal. Why do we do it that way? Yeah, it's it, there's not an easier way to do it, right?",
  "hw4-1crcl": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, estimated with ideal body weight. The answer is seventy-one point six milliliters per minute. The stem asks for the creatinine clearance of a female patient with a serum creatinine, so the line is Cockcroft-Gault, times zero point eight five because she is female. The ideal body weight from the first part, fifty-four point seven kilograms, goes in the numerator. The actual weight of fifty-eight kilograms is set aside, because the question says to use ideal body weight. The top is one hundred forty minus thirty-eight, which is one hundred two, times fifty-four point seven kilograms, giving five thousand five hundred seventy-nine point four. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point nine two milligrams per deciliter, which is sixty-six point two four. The ratio is eighty-four point two. Times zero point eight five gives seventy-one point six milliliters per minute. The Cockcroft-Gault constants carry the units, so creatinine clearance is reported in milliliters per minute, the unit of the glomerular filtration rate it estimates.",
  "hw4-2d": "Renal clearance, in liters per hour. The answer is one point six nine eight liters per hour. Drug is recovered in the urine after an I V dose, so the line is Cl R equals f e, the fraction excreted unchanged, times Cl T, the total clearance. The dose, fifteen milligrams per kilogram times seventy kilograms, is one thousand fifty milligrams. So f e is seven hundred thirty-five milligrams divided by one thousand fifty milligrams, which is zero point seven zero, with no units. Total clearance from part b, k times V D with the six-hour half-life and twenty-one liters, is two point four two five five liters per hour. The fraction found unchanged in urine is the share of the elimination the kidney did. So Cl R is zero point seven zero times two point four two five five liters per hour, which is one point six nine eight liters per hour. In milliliters per minute that is twenty-eight point three milliliters per minute, the form set beside the filtration rate in part e. Renal clearance, one point six nine eight liters per hour, sits below the total clearance.",
  "m4-n-clr": "Renal clearance, in liters per hour. The answer is zero point seven eight liters per hour. Unchanged drug is recovered after a rapid I V injection, so the line is Cl R equals f e times Cl T, with both factors found first. The fraction excreted unchanged, f e, is three hundred milligrams divided by five hundred milligrams, which is zero point six. The elimination rate constant is zero point six nine three divided by eight hours, which is zero point zero eight six six two five per hour. Total clearance is that rate constant times fifteen liters, which is one point three liters per hour. Cl R is zero point six times one point three liters per hour, which is zero point seven eight liters per hour, smaller than the total clearance. Carrying the unrounded one point two nine nine three seven five liters per hour gives zero point seven seven nine six liters per hour, which rounds to the same value. She said, zero point six or F E times one liter per hour, one point three liters per hour. OK. So I'm getting zero point seven eight liters per hour.",
  "m4-n-crcl": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, estimated with ideal body weight. The answer is fifty-eight point one milliliters per minute. The stem asks for creatinine clearance using the ideal body weight of a female, so the line is Cockcroft-Gault times zero point eight five. Ideal body weight comes first, because the line wants ideal weight, not the sixty-one kilogram actual weight. One hundred sixty-five centimeters divided by two point five four centimeters per inch is sixty-four point nine six inches, taken as sixty-five inches, which is five inches over five feet. The female ideal body weight is forty-five point five plus two point three times five, which is fifty-seven kilograms. The top is one hundred forty minus forty-five, which is ninety-five, times fifty-seven kilograms, giving five thousand four hundred fifteen. The bottom is seventy-two times one point one milligrams per deciliter, which is seventy-nine point two. The ratio is sixty-eight point three seven. Times zero point eight five gives fifty-eight point one one milliliters per minute, rounded to fifty-eight point one milliliters per minute. The result is well below the normal one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute.",
  "ws4-1-crcl": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, of a male patient. The answer is one hundred twenty-three point five milliliters per minute. The stem asks for creatinine clearance and gives age, serum creatinine and height, so the line is Cockcroft-Gault. Ideal body weight comes first, because her keys use ideal body weight, not the one hundred sixty pounds given. Sixty-eight inches minus sixty inches is eight inches over five feet. The male ideal body weight is fifty plus two point three times eight, which is sixty-eight point four kilograms. The patient is male, so the zero point eight five factor is not applied. The top is one hundred forty minus thirty-six, which is one hundred four, times sixty-eight point four kilograms, giving seven thousand one hundred thirteen point six. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point eight milligrams per deciliter, which is fifty-seven point six. The ratio is one hundred twenty-three point five milliliters per minute. A thirty-six-year-old man with a normal serum creatinine has a normal glomerular filtration rate, about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute, so the value fits. Far below one hundred or above one hundred forty would mean a slipped number.",
  "ws4-2-2": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, of a female patient. The answer is one hundred twenty point two milliliters per minute. The stem asks for creatinine clearance of a female and gives age, serum creatinine and height, so the line is Cockcroft-Gault with the zero point eight five factor. Ideal body weight comes first, and the one hundred forty-three pound weight is not used. Five feet six inches is six inches over five feet, read straight off the height. The female ideal body weight is forty-five point five plus two point three times six, which is fifty-nine point three kilograms. The top is one hundred forty minus thirty-seven, which is one hundred three, times fifty-nine point three kilograms, giving six thousand one hundred seven point nine. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point six milligrams per deciliter, which is forty-three point two. The ratio is one hundred forty-one point four. Women produce less creatinine per kilogram of body weight, so this is multiplied by zero point eight five, giving one hundred twenty point two milliliters per minute. Forgetting the zero point eight five gives a value about a sixth too high.",
  "ws4-4-2": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, of a female patient. The answer is seventy-nine point one nine milliliters per minute. For a female, the line is Cockcroft-Gault with the zero point eight five factor, using ideal body weight, not the eighty-two kilogram weight. One hundred seventy centimeters divided by two point five four centimeters per inch is sixty-six point nine three inches. That is six point nine three inches over five feet, rounded to seven inches, as her keys do. The female ideal body weight is forty-five point five plus two point three times seven, which is sixty-one point six kilograms. The top is one hundred forty minus forty-two, which is ninety-eight, times sixty-one point six kilograms, giving six thousand thirty-six point eight. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point nine milligrams per deciliter, which is sixty-four point eight. The ratio is ninety-three point one six, and times zero point eight five it is seventy-nine point one nine milliliters per minute. Keeping six point nine three inches gives seventy-eight point nine eight milliliters per minute; both are accepted. Skipping the zero point eight five gives a value about a sixth too high.",
  "x3-b-crclm": "The creatinine clearance, in milliliters per minute, of a male patient using ideal body weight. The answer is one hundred fifteen point seven milliliters per minute. The line is Cockcroft-Gault with the male ideal body weight and no zero point eight five factor. Five feet eight inches is eight inches over five feet. The male ideal body weight is fifty plus two point three times eight, which is sixty-eight point four kilograms; the one hundred fifty-four pound actual weight is not used. The top is one hundred forty minus forty-five, which is ninety-five, times sixty-eight point four kilograms, giving six thousand four hundred ninety-eight. The bottom is seventy-two times zero point seven eight milligrams per deciliter, which is fifty-six point one six. The ratio is one hundred fifteen point seven one, or one hundred fifteen point seven milliliters per minute. A female with the same numbers gives ninety-one point nine milliliters per minute, because both the ideal body weight constant and the zero point eight five factor change. She said, So, a for males, the ideal body weight is fifty plus two point three times the inches over five feet.",
  "x3-b-mech120": "The probable mechanism of renal excretion at a renal clearance of about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. The answer is filtration only. A renal clearance at the glomerular filtration rate, about one hundred twenty milliliters per minute, is what filtration alone produces. Nothing is added beyond what is filtered and nothing filtered is missing, so no second process is inferred. A value a few milliliters per minute either side, such as one hundred nineteen or one hundred twenty-one milliliters per minute, is read the same way. Choosing active secretion reads secretion into a value that does not exceed the filtration rate; secretion is inferred only above it, as at three hundred or three hundred fifty milliliters per minute. Choosing tubular reabsorption reads reabsorption into a value that is not below the filtration rate; reabsorption is inferred only below it, as at forty-five milliliters per minute. She said, And I'm saying one hundred twenty. If it's one hundred nineteen, one hundred twenty-one, OK, let's call that filtration. If it's two hundred fifty, then let's assume that we've got some active secretion going on.",
  "x3-b-mechlhr": "The probable mechanism of renal excretion for a renal clearance of twenty-one point zero two liters per hour. The answer is filtration with active secretion. Renal clearance is compared with the glomerular filtration rate in the same units. Twenty-one point zero two liters per hour, times one thousand and divided by sixty, is three hundred fifty point three milliliters per minute, well above one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. A renal clearance above the filtration rate means drug is being added to the lumen by active tubular secretion as well as by filtration. Choosing filtration only treats twenty-one point zero two liters per hour as close to the filtration rate without converting it. Choosing reabsorption compares the number twenty-one point zero two with one hundred twenty in different units; one hundred twenty milliliters per minute is seven point two liters per hour, and reabsorption would put renal clearance below that. She said, So, here we are comparing this to just G F R, right? So we're comparing the renal clearance to one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. So if it's more than one hundred twenty, we assume active secretion, less than one hundred twenty, we assume um reabsorption.",
  "cq3-3": "What sets the time to the maximum concentration after an oral dose. The answer is that the time to the maximum concentration depends on the rate constants for absorption and elimination. T max equals the natural log of the ratio k a over k, divided by the difference k a minus k. Only the two rate constants appear in that equation. The dose, F and V D multiply the whole curve, so they scale its height without moving its peak in time. Changing the dose moves C max and A U C in proportion and leaves t max unchanged, because the dose multiplies the absorption rate and the elimination rate by the same amount. Choosing a longer time to peak with a larger dose reads the peak as a level the dose must fill; a larger dose raises C max in proportion and leaves t max where it was. Choosing independence from the rate constants reverses the relation, because the two rate constants are the only quantities t max depends on.",
  "cq3-4": "What is true at the maximum concentration after an oral dose. The answer is that the rate of drug absorption equals the rate of drug elimination. At the peak the concentration is momentarily not changing. The amount in the body changes at the rate in minus the rate out, so at the peak that difference is zero and drug enters exactly as fast as it leaves. Both processes are first order: absorption runs at k a times the amount at the absorption site, and elimination at k times the amount in the body. The first falls as the second rises, so they cross once, at t max. Elimination faster than absorption is the tempting wrong answer; it describes every moment after the peak, the falling limb. Absorption faster than elimination describes every moment before the peak, the rising limb. Depletion of the absorption site is also wrong, because absorption is still running at t max.",
  "cq3-5": "The time of the peak after one oral dose, t max, in hours. The answer is two point six hours. With absorption and elimination half-lives given and the peak time asked, the line is t max equals the natural log of the ratio k a over k, divided by the difference k a minus k. The one thousand milligram dose, the eighty-three percent bioavailability and the eighteen liter volume are set aside; they would be needed for C max, not t max. The equation takes rate constants, so k a is zero point six nine three divided by zero point eight hours, which is zero point eight six six three per hour. And k is zero point six nine three divided by five point six hours, which is zero point one two three eight per hour. Their ratio is seven, whose natural log is one point nine four five nine, and their difference is zero point seven four two five per hour. So t max is one point nine four five nine divided by zero point seven four two five per hour, which is two point six two one hours, rounded to two point six hours.",
  "e2r-12": "What is true during the absorption phase after an oral dose. The answer is that the rate of drug absorption is faster than the rate of drug elimination. Before the peak, more drug enters from the absorption site each hour than is eliminated, so the concentration rises. That rising limb is the absorption phase. At the peak, t max, the two rates are equal. Choosing equal rates takes one instant for a phase; the rates are equal only at t max, and the absorption phase is the whole stretch before it. Choosing elimination faster than absorption describes the post-absorption phase after the peak, when the concentration falls although some drug is still being absorbed. Depletion of the absorption site names the elimination phase, after absorption is complete.",
  "e2r-3e": "C max, the peak after the first oral dose, is forty seven point eight milligrams per liter. A first oral dose calls for the single oral dose equation at t max. F times D zero is nine hundred milligrams, from parts c and d; the eight hour interval is set aside for a first dose. Seventy five minutes is one point two five hours, so k a is zero point five five four four per hour. k, clearance divided by V D, is zero point one six five three per hour. t max, the natural log of the ratio k a divided by k, all divided by k a minus k, is three point one one zero hours. The front factor, F times k a times D zero divided by the product of V D and the difference k a minus k, is one hundred thirteen point nine nine milligrams per liter. The bracket, e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t, is zero point four one nine seven at t max, so C max is forty seven point eight four, rounded to forty seven point eight milligrams per liter.",
  "e2r-6": "What a two-fold increase in oral dose does. The correct choices are increased A U C and increased C max. A U C equals F times D zero divided by clearance, so the dose sits in the numerator and nothing else changes: doubling the dose doubles the area. The dose also multiplies the whole oral curve, so the peak doubles along with every other point while its time stays the same. t max does not move, because t max, the natural log of the ratio k a divided by k, all divided by the difference k a minus k, holds only the two rate constants. Her slide Changing Dose shows A U C rising in proportion to the dose. Choosing increased t max reads a bigger dose as taking longer to reach its peak; the dose scales the curve up without shifting it in time. Choosing decreased t max lets the dose move the time of the peak. Choosing decreased A U C or decreased C max reverses the direction.",
  "fig-oral-1": "The route and kinetics that a curve with a clear peak shows. The answer is an extravascular dose, first order in and first order out. The concentration starts at zero, rises to a clear peak and then falls away. A rise means drug is still entering, so the dose was not placed directly into the blood. Before the peak, absorption is faster than elimination; at the peak the two rates are equal; after the peak, elimination is the larger. The smooth curved rise and fall are both first-order processes. An I V bolus places the whole dose in the blood at once, so its highest concentration is at time zero and the curve only falls. A constant-rate infusion climbs to a plateau and stays there while it runs; it does not peak and decline until it is switched off. She said, if I give you a curve on the exam and it looks like this. where there is a clear peak. we go up, we peak, we come back down, then you, I want you to identify that as an oral input. First order in, first order out.",
  "fr-7m": "Which input and output pattern belongs to each route. Instantaneous input with first-order output is the I V bolus. First-order input with first-order output is the conventional oral dose. Zero-order input with first-order output is the I V infusion. All three routes share first-order elimination; they differ in how the drug is put into the body. A bolus puts the whole dose into the blood at once, so the curve starts at C zero and falls. Absorption from the gut is first order: fast while much drug is at the absorption site, slower as it empties, so the curve rises to a peak and then falls. An infusion pump delivers a constant rate, which is zero order, so the level rises until rate out equals rate in, the steady state. She said, So, instantaneous in, first order out is our bolus, first order in, first order out is our oral, our conventional oral, and then zero order in, first order out is our I V infusion.",
  "hw5-1b": "C max, the peak after one oral dose, is twelve point zero four milligrams per liter. A single oral dose with bioavailability, two half-lives and a volume calls for the single oral dose equation at t max, which part a gives as two point nine zero hours. The sixty minute absorption half-life is one hour, so k a is zero point six nine three zero per hour; k is zero point six nine three over five hours, zero point one three eight six per hour. The numerator, F of zero point nine times six hundred milligrams times k a, is three hundred seventy four point two two milligrams per hour. The denominator, thirty liters times the difference k a minus k, is sixteen point six three two liters per hour, so the coefficient is twenty two point five zero zero milligrams per liter. At t max the elimination term, e to the minus k t, is zero point six six nine zero, and the absorption term, e to the minus k a t, is zero point one three four zero; the difference is zero point five three five zero. The product is twelve point zero four milligrams per liter.",
  "m5-c13": "What is true in the post-absorption phase after an oral dose. The answer: the rate of drug elimination is faster than the rate of drug absorption. After the peak, the falling amount left in the gut can no longer keep pace with elimination, so the concentration declines. Absorption has not stopped; both processes are still running, which is why this phase is named separately from the one that follows. Choosing drug at the absorption site has become depleted names the next phase, the complete elimination phase, governed by k alone. Choosing absorption faster than elimination describes the absorption phase before the peak. Choosing equal rates takes the peak for a phase; equal rates hold at one instant only, t max. She said, once all the drug has been absorbed, Then we are fully into the complete elimination phase.",
  "m5-c16": "The effect of doubling an oral dose on the time to peak. The answer: the time to peak does not change. t max equals the natural log of the ratio k a divided by k, all divided by the difference k a minus k, so it contains only the absorption and elimination rate constants. Those rate constants are properties of the drug and the patient, and neither depends on the dose. The rates of absorption and elimination both rise with the dose, because each is proportional to an amount that is now larger, so the moment they balance stays put. The taller curve peaks directly above the old one. Choosing it doubles treats the peak as a level to be filled, so twice the drug should take twice as long. Choosing it is halved confuses a rate with a rate constant: more drug speeds the absorption rate in milligrams per hour, but k a is unchanged. She said, If I increase the dose, what happens to T-Max? Nothing, right? ... T-Max is independent or dependent of dose. Independent",
  "m5-n04": "C max, the peak after one oral dose, is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter. A single oral dose with bioavailability and a V D calls for the single oral dose equation at t max. Forty five minutes is zero point seven five hours, so k a is zero point nine two four per hour; k is zero point two three one per hour. t max is two point zero zero zero four hours. The prefactor, F times k a times D zero divided by the product of V D and the difference k a minus k, is twenty five point seven five eight milligrams per liter. The bracket, e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t, at the peak is zero point four seven two four eight, and the product is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter. Leaving V D out is the commonest error. She said, zero point eight five times five hundred times zero point nine two four divided by twenty two, divided by left parentheses zero point nine two four minus zero point two three one ... And I am getting twelve point one seven. Milligrams per liter.",
  "m5-n13": "C max, the peak plasma concentration after one oral dose, is thirteen point one three milligrams per liter. A dose by mouth with bioavailability, two half-lives and a V D calls for the single oral dose equation at t max. Ninety minutes is one point five hours, so k a is zero point four six two per hour; k is zero point one three eight six per hour. t max comes first; she uses three point seven hours. The prefactor, F times k a times D zero divided by the product of V D and the difference k a minus k, is thirty one point four two nine milligrams per liter. The bracket, e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t, at three point seven hours is zero point four one seven eight three, and the product is thirteen point one three milligrams per liter. V D plays no part in t max, so it is the parameter most often dropped in the second stage, the C max step. She said, Do not forget the volume of distribution. Our volume distribution is twenty liters ... And I'm getting thirteen-ish milligrams per liter.",
  "ws5-3-4": "C max, the peak after one oral dose, is thirty point three two milligrams per liter. The drug is given orally and C max is asked, so the single oral dose equation is evaluated at t max. The dose, F and V D sit inside the coefficient from part three, which is thirty four point one nine milligrams per liter. The ten hour elimination half-life gives k of zero point zero six nine three per hour, and the zero point two five hour absorption half-life gives k a of two point seven seven two per hour. t max is one point three six hours; absorption is forty times faster than elimination, so the peak comes early. At that time the elimination term, e to the minus k t, is zero point nine one zero and the absorption term, e to the minus k a t, is zero point zero two three, so the bracket, their difference, is zero point eight eight seven. The coefficient times the bracket is thirty point three, or thirty point three two milligrams per liter unrounded. The bracket is a difference of two numbers between zero and one, so C max lies below the coefficient.",
  "x3-b-cmaxq3": "C max, the maximum plasma concentration after one oral dose, is thirty three point three milligrams per liter. A single oral dose with two half-lives calls for the single oral dose equation at t max. k a is zero point eight six six two five per hour and k is zero point one two three seven five per hour, each zero point six nine three over its half-life in hours. For t max, the natural log of seven, the ratio of the rate constants, is divided by their difference, zero point seven four two five per hour, giving two point six two one hours. The constant in front, F times k a times D zero divided by the product of V D and the difference k a minus k, is fifty three point eight zero milligrams per liter. The bracket, elimination term minus absorption term, is zero point six one nine seven. The product, thirty three point three four milligrams per liter, rounds to thirty three point three. She said, if you are asked for C-Max, and I will ask you for CMax, you must find TMax first. Find T-Max before you find CMax, OK?",
  "x3-b-polltmax": "What happens to t max when an oral dose is increased. The answer: no change in t max. For a single oral dose, t max equals the natural log of the ratio k a divided by k, all divided by the difference k a minus k, so it depends solely on k a and k. A larger dose changes neither rate constant. C max and A U C rise in proportion to the dose, while the time of the peak stays where it was. The rates of absorption and elimination do rise, but by the same proportion, because both are first order, so they become equal at the same time as before. Choosing a decrease in t max reads the faster rate of absorption from a larger dose as an earlier peak. Choosing an increase in t max treats the peak as a fixed target that a larger dose takes longer to reach. She said, If I increase the dose, what happens to T-Max? Nothing, right? ... T-Max is independent or dependent of dose. Independent, right?",
  "cq4-2": "The maximum steady-state concentration, the peak just after a dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is one hundred fifty one point one milligrams per liter. I V bolus injections every eight hours with a maximum steady-state concentration call for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. The dose and the volume are per kilogram, so both are scaled to the patient first: seventeen milligrams per kilogram times seventy five kilograms is one thousand two hundred seventy five milligrams, and V D is eleven point two five liters. C zero is one hundred thirteen point three milligrams per liter. k is zero point six nine three over four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. Eight hours is two half-lives, so e to the minus k tau is zero point two five zero, a quarter left at each dose. One hundred thirteen point three milligrams per liter divided by zero point seven five zero, which is one minus zero point two five zero, is one hundred fifty one point one milligrams per liter, a third above the first-dose peak.",
  "cq4-3": "The best way to raise the steady-state concentration in a dosing regimen. The answer: decrease the dosing interval. Dosing more often puts the next dose in before as much of the last has left, so the plateau rises. A shorter interval leaves more of each dose when the next arrives, so the accumulation factor, one divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau, rises. C max, C min and the average at steady state all go up, and the trough sits closer to the peak. Her rule is that a regimen can change only the size of the dose and the dosing interval. Choosing increase the dosing interval is the direction many pick; a longer interval gives elimination more time, so the level falls. Choosing increase the clearance or decrease the elimination half-life treats a property of the drug in the patient as a dosing choice, and either change would lower the level.",
  "cq4-4": "The plasma concentration four hours after the second infusion stops is ten point six milligrams per liter. The two infusions are worked separately and added. From the first start, the infusions end at two and ten hours, and the level is asked at fourteen hours. R, the infusion rate, is two hundred milligrams per hour. k is zero point one seven three three per hour, and Cl T, k times twenty liters, is three point four six five liters per hour. Each infusion ends at R divided by Cl T, fifty seven point seven two milligrams per liter, times the quantity one minus zero point seven zero seven one: sixteen point nine zero milligrams per liter. The first has declined twelve hours, three half-lives, leaving two point one one milligrams per liter. The second has declined four hours, one half-life, leaving eight point four five milligrams per liter. The sum, ten point five six, rounds to ten point six milligrams per liter. Leaving out the first infusion gives eight point four five milligrams per liter, too low; treating each infusion as a four hundred milligram bolus into twenty liters gives twelve point five milligrams per liter, too high.",
  "cq4-5": "What a larger dose does in an oral regimen given at regular intervals. The answer: increased steady-state C max. Every concentration in the regimen scales with the dose, so the steady-state peak rises in proportion. Her slide Altering Dose says increasing the dose increases steady-state concentrations and the peak-to-trough fluctuation. The dose does not move t max, k a or clearance. Choosing increased steady-state t max reads a larger dose as a slower climb to the peak; t max at steady state depends on k a, k and tau only. Choosing decreased clearance takes the amount cleared per hour for the clearance: a larger dose raises the amount removed per hour, but Cl itself does not change. Choosing decreased rate of absorption treats k a as adjustable by the dose; a larger dose means more drug absorbed per hour at the same k a.",
  "cq4-8": "The oral dose, D zero, in milligrams, for an average plasma concentration of twenty five milligrams per liter. The answer is seven hundred twenty three milligrams. Dosing by mouth every eight hours to a target average concentration calls for the average steady-state line: the average equals F times D zero divided by the product of clearance and tau, rearranged for D zero. Clearance is given directly, so the absorption half-life of ninety minutes and the V D of twenty five liters are set aside; the average does not depend on k a or V D. Twenty five milligrams per liter times three point zero liters per hour times eight hours is six hundred milligrams, the amount cleared in one interval at the target. Dividing by F of zero point eight three gives seven hundred twenty two point nine milligrams, rounded to seven hundred twenty three. Multiplying by F instead of dividing gives four hundred ninety eight milligrams, less than the six hundred milligrams that has to be absorbed, which cannot be right.",
  "e2r-3a": "The maximum steady-state concentration, the peak just after a dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is one hundred nine point one milligrams per liter. I V bolus doses every eight hours with a maximum steady-state concentration call for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. Twelve milligrams per kilogram times seventy five kilograms is nine hundred milligrams, and V D is eleven point two five liters. Clearance is given instead of a half-life, so k is one point eight six liters per hour divided by eleven point two five liters, zero point one six five three three per hour. C zero, nine hundred milligrams over eleven point two five liters, is eighty milligrams per liter. e to the minus k tau, the fraction left after one interval, is zero point two six six four. Eighty milligrams per liter divided by one minus zero point two six six four, which is zero point seven three three six, is one hundred nine point zero five, rounded to one hundred nine point one milligrams per liter. The steady-state peak must exceed the eighty milligrams per liter first-dose peak and stay below twice it.",
  "e2r-3b": "The dose, D zero, in milligrams, for an average steady-state concentration of about eighty micrograms per milliliter. The answer is one thousand one hundred ninety point four milligrams. An average steady-state concentration calls for the average line: F times D zero divided by the product of Cl T and tau, with F of one for an I V bolus, solved for D zero. The average needs only clearance and the interval, because over one interval at steady state the drug given equals the drug cleared. So V D, and the old dose of twelve milligrams per kilogram, are set aside. Eighty micrograms per milliliter is eighty milligrams per liter, and that conversion comes first. Eighty milligrams per liter times one point eight six liters per hour is one hundred forty eight point eight milligrams per hour, the drug cleared each hour at the target. Times eight hours, that is one thousand one hundred ninety point four milligrams to replace each interval. For the seventy five kilogram patient, that is fifteen point eight seven milligrams per kilogram.",
  "e2r-3f": "The dosing interval, tau, in hours, for an average steady-state concentration of eighty micrograms per milliliter with the oral dose from part d. The answer is every six hours. The average steady-state concentration equals F times D zero divided by the product of Cl T and tau, so solved for tau, the interval equals F times D zero divided by the product of Cl T and the target average. F of zero point seven five and the dose of one thousand two hundred milligrams come from parts c and d; V D is set aside, because the average uses clearance only. Eighty micrograms per milliliter is eighty milligrams per liter. The top, F times D zero, is nine hundred milligrams absorbed per dose. The bottom, one point eight six liters per hour times eighty milligrams per liter, is one hundred forty eight point eight milligrams per hour cleared at the target. Milligrams divided by milligrams per hour leaves hours: six point zero five hours. A dosing interval is given as a practical clock interval, so six point zero five hours becomes every six hours.",
  "e2r-4": "The plasma concentration four hours after the second infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is eight point nine milligrams per liter. Two short infusions: the end level of each, its decline since it stopped, then the sum. The first runs from zero to two hours, the second from eight to ten hours, and the level is asked at fourteen hours, so the first has declined for twelve hours and the second for four. k is zero point one eight four eight per hour; Cl T, k times twenty two liters, is four point zero six six liters per hour; R, the infusion rate, is two hundred milligrams per hour. Each infusion ends at R divided by Cl T, forty nine point one nine milligrams per liter, times the quantity one minus e to the minus k times two hours: fifteen point two zero milligrams per liter. The first leaves one point six five five milligrams per liter, and the second leaves seven point two five eight milligrams per liter. The sum, eight point nine one three, rounds to eight point nine milligrams per liter. The concentrations add because elimination is first order.",
  "e2r-9": "What follows a shorter dosing interval with the same dose. The answer: increased steady-state concentrations. Her slide Altering Dosing Interval says decreasing the dosing interval increases steady-state concentrations. Each dose arrives before as much of the last has left, so C max, C min and the average at steady state all rise. Choosing increased fluctuations between peak and trough takes the bullet for increasing the interval; a shorter interval leaves less time for the level to fall, so the trough sits closer to the peak and fluctuation decreases. Choosing increased patient compliance reverses her slide: more frequent doses decrease patient compliance. Choosing decreased M T C treats the minimum toxic concentration, which belongs to the drug, as something the regimen moves.",
  "fr-12c": "The concentration four hours after the second infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is two point two milligrams per liter. Each infusion ends at four point four five milligrams per liter, from part a, and then declines by first order from its own end; the two are added. The first runs from zero to two hours, the second from six to eight hours, and the level is asked at twelve hours, so the first has declined for ten hours and the second for four. k is zero point two three one per hour, from the three hour half-life. The first leaves four point four five milligrams per liter times zero point zero nine nine three, which is zero point four four two milligrams per liter. The second leaves four point four five milligrams per liter times zero point three nine six nine, which is one point seven six six milligrams per liter. The sum is two point two zero eight milligrams per liter, rounded to two point two milligrams per liter. She said, So remember, once the infusion ends, it's just first order elimination",
  "fr-6a": "The maximum steady-state concentration for repeated I V bolus doses every twelve hours. The answer is twenty one point four milligrams per liter. C zero, the first-dose peak, is three hundred milligrams divided by sixteen liters, which is eighteen point seven five milligrams per liter. k is zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three two five per hour, and for tau of twelve hours, e to the minus k tau is zero point one two five one. C max at steady state equals C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau, which is twenty one point four three milligrams per liter. Choosing eighteen point seven milligrams per liter stops at the first dose; at steady state each dose lands on what is left of the earlier ones. Choosing thirty two point six milligrams per liter overstates accumulation: twelve hours is three half-lives, so only twelve point five percent is left and the factor is one point one four. She said, You should have gotten twenty one point four milligrams per liter. What is the minimum? At steady state. Two point seven, OK.",
  "fr-6b": "The minimum plasma concentration at steady state, the trough just before the next dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is two point seven milligrams per liter. The steady-state peak from part a, twenty one point four milligrams per liter, is known, so the trough is that peak times e to the minus k tau, one interval of first-order decline. The volume and the dose are set aside, because the peak is already known. k is zero point six nine three divided by four hours, which is zero point one seven three two five per hour. k tau, with tau of twelve hours, is two point zero seven nine, and e to the minus two point zero seven nine is zero point one two five one. Twenty one point four milligrams per liter times zero point one two five one is two point six seven seven milligrams per liter, rounded to two point seven milligrams per liter. Twelve hours is three half-lives, and three half-lives leave one eighth of the peak. She said, What is the minimum? At steady state. Two point seven, OK. Anybody else get two point seven?",
  "hwo5-2b": "The maximum plasma concentration at steady state, the peak just after a dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is ninety point nine two milligrams per liter. Multiple I V bolus injections every eight hours at steady state call for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. The forty eight hours of dosing is twelve half-lives, so steady state is reached; it is not needed for the number. For the seventy eight kilogram weight, the dose is one thousand one hundred seventy milligrams and V D is seventeen point one six liters. k is zero point six nine three over four hours, which is zero point one seven three two five per hour. C zero is sixty eight point one eight one eight milligrams per liter, the rise each dose produces. Eight hours is two half-lives, so e to the minus k tau is zero point two five zero one, a quarter left at each dose. Sixty eight point one eight one eight milligrams per liter divided by zero point seven four nine nine, which is one minus zero point two five zero one, is ninety point nine two milligrams per liter.",
  "m6-n03": "The maximum concentration at steady state, the peak just after a dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is fifty three point three milligrams per liter. Repeated I V bolus injections with the steady-state maximum asked call for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. C zero is ten milligrams per kilogram over zero point two five liters per kilogram, which is forty milligrams per liter; the kilograms cancel, so the weight is set aside. k is zero point one seven three three per hour, k tau over eight hours is one point three eight six, and e to the minus one point three eight six is zero point two five, the fraction of each dose left at the end of the interval. Forty milligrams per liter divided by zero point seven five, which is one minus zero point two five, is fifty three point three milligrams per liter. A result below forty milligrams per liter means the factor was multiplied instead of divided. She said, fifty three point three? OK, fifty three point three milligrams per liter, which is greater than forty, right? That is what we expect.",
  "m6-n04": "The minimum concentration at steady state, the trough just before the next dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is thirteen point three milligrams per liter. The steady-state peak of fifty three point three milligrams per liter is given, so the trough is that peak times e to the minus k tau, one interval of first-order decline. The volume, the dose and the weight are set aside, because they are already inside the given peak. k has not changed, zero point one seven three three per hour, and k tau is one point three eight six; the eight hour interval is two half-lives, so the peak is halved twice. Fifty three point three times zero point two five is thirteen point three milligrams per liter. She said, So we're starting at fifty three point three milligrams per liter, E to the minus. Our K has not changed, zero point six nine three divided by four ... the minimum occurs at eight hours.",
  "m6b-n03": "The plasma concentration four hours after the second infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is thirteen point three four milligrams per liter. After each infusion stops only first-order decline remains, so each contributes its end level of seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter times e to the minus k t, and the two are added. The dose and V D are set aside. The infusions end at two hours and at eight hours, and the time asked is twelve hours. The first has declined for ten hours: k t is one point five, so it leaves three point eight six milligrams per liter. The second has declined for four hours: k t is zero point six, so it leaves nine point four eight milligrams per liter. The sum is thirteen point three four milligrams per liter. The error she warns about is using four hours for both. She said, The answer that I'm looking for for this one will be thirteen point three three milligrams per liter.",
  "m6b-n05": "The plasma concentration four hours after the end of the second infusion, in milligrams per liter. The answer is eleven point zero four milligrams per liter. After each infusion stops only first-order elimination is left, so each contributes its end level of twenty two point two four milligrams per liter times e to the minus k t, and the two are added. The dose and V D are set aside. k is zero point six nine three over three hours, which is zero point two three one per hour. The infusions end at two hours and at eight hours, so at twelve hours the second has declined for four hours and the first for ten. The second leaves eight point eight three milligrams per liter and the first leaves two point two one milligrams per liter, so the sum is eleven point zero four milligrams per liter. Using four hours, or twelve, as t for both doses is the error. She said, So the twelve comes from part B says the concentration four hours after the end of the second infusion. Second infusion ends right here at time eight, so that's where the twelve comes from.",
  "ws6-b3d": "The maximum steady-state plasma concentration, the peak just after a dose, is one hundred six point six eight milligrams per liter. An I V bolus every six hours with the steady-state maximum asked calls for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. V D is twenty five percent of the eighty kilogram weight, twenty liters. C zero is twenty milligrams per kilogram divided by zero point two five liters per kilogram, eighty milligrams per liter. k is clearance divided by V D: four point six two liters per hour divided by twenty liters, zero point two three one per hour. Over six hours k tau is one point three eight six, and e to the minus one point three eight six, the fraction left at the end of an interval, is zero point two five zero one, so zero point seven four nine nine is eliminated. Eighty milligrams per liter divided by zero point seven four nine nine is one hundred six point six eight milligrams per liter, an accumulation factor of one point three three. The steady-state peak must exceed the first-dose peak of eighty milligrams per liter.",
  "ws6-b4a": "The I V dose, D zero, in milligrams, for an average steady-state concentration of twenty five milligrams per liter, to the nearest ten milligrams. The answer is five hundred fifty milligrams. I V bolus injections every four hours with a target average call for the average line, F times D zero divided by the product of V D, k and tau, rearranged for D zero, with F of one for an I V dose. One hundred ninety two pounds divided by two point two is eighty seven point two seven kilograms, so V D is twenty six point two liters. k is zero point six nine three over three point three hours, which is zero point two one per hour. D zero is twenty five milligrams per liter times twenty six point two liters times zero point two one per hour times four hours, which is five hundred fifty point two milligrams; the liters and the hours cancel, leaving milligrams. Rounded to the nearest ten milligrams, the dose is five hundred fifty milligrams.",
  "ws6-b4c": "The maximum steady-state plasma concentration for the five hundred fifty milligram dose, in milligrams per liter. The answer is thirty six point nine five milligrams per liter. I V bolus injections every four hours with the steady-state maximum asked call for C zero divided by the quantity one minus e to the minus k tau. V D is twenty six point two liters, so C zero is five hundred fifty milligrams over twenty six point two liters, which is twenty one milligrams per liter. k is zero point six nine three over three point three hours, which is zero point two one per hour, and k tau over four hours is zero point eight four, a pure number. Four hours is only one point two half-lives, so forty three percent of each dose remains, and one minus e to the minus k tau is zero point five six eight three. Twenty one milligrams per liter divided by zero point five six eight three is thirty six point nine five milligrams per liter, an accumulation factor of one point seven six. The steady-state peak must exceed the twenty one milligrams per liter first-dose peak.",
  "ws6-i1-1b": "The plasma concentration six hours after the second infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is nine point zero two milligrams per liter. Each infusion contributes its end level, twenty eight point eight six milligrams per liter from part a, times e to the minus k t, and the two are added because elimination is first order. V D is twenty liters, so k is four point six two liters per hour over twenty liters, which is zero point two three one per hour. The infusions end at three hours and at nine hours, and the time asked is fifteen hours, so the second has declined for six hours and the first for twelve hours. Six hours is two half-lives, leaving a quarter, which is seven point two one five milligrams per liter. Twelve hours is four half-lives, leaving a sixteenth, which is one point eight zero four milligrams per liter. The sum is nine point zero two milligrams per liter. The error to avoid is using the same time for both infusions.",
  "ws6-i1-2i": "The oral dose, D zero, in milligrams, for an average steady-state concentration of twenty five milligrams per liter. The answer is six hundred milligrams. Dosing orally every twelve hours to a target average calls for the average line, F times D zero divided by the product of V D, k and tau, rearranged so D zero equals the average times V D times k times tau, divided by F. The old five hundred milligram dose, the two weeks and the absorption half-life are set aside. V D is zero point two four liters per kilogram times eighty kilograms, which is nineteen point two liters, and k is zero point zero nine four nine per hour. The target times the volume, k and the twelve hour interval is five hundred forty six point six milligrams, the amount to be absorbed each interval. Dividing by F of zero point nine gives six hundred seven point three six milligrams, which her key rounds to six hundred milligrams. Dividing by F makes the oral dose larger than the amount absorbed, because only ninety percent reaches the circulation.",
  "ws6-i3-1b": "The plasma concentration four hours after the second infusion stops, in milligrams per liter. The answer is six point five four milligrams per liter. Each infusion contributes its end level times e to the minus k t, and the two are added because elimination is first order. The end level from part a is ten point four six milligrams per liter, so the clearance of two point eight liters per hour is not needed again. k is zero point six nine three divided by four hours, zero point one seven three two five per hour. The first infusion runs from zero to two hours, the second from eight to ten hours, and four hours after the second ends is fourteen hours. The first has declined for twelve hours, three half-lives, leaving one eighth of ten point four six milligrams per liter, which is one point three one milligrams per liter. The second has declined for four hours, one half-life, leaving half, five point two three milligrams per liter. The sum is six point five four milligrams per liter. The error to avoid is using the same time for both infusions.",
  "ws6-i3-2e": "The oral dose, D zero, in milligrams, for an average steady-state concentration of thirty milligrams per liter every eight hours. The answer is seven hundred fifty milligrams. The average steady-state concentration equals F times D zero divided by the product of Cl T and tau, so D zero equals the average times Cl T times tau, divided by F. The current dose of five hundred milligrams is set aside, because the dose is what is asked. Thirty milligrams per liter times two point five liters per hour times eight hours is six hundred milligrams, the amount that must be absorbed each interval; the liters and hours cancel to leave milligrams. The capsule is oral, so F applies: six hundred milligrams divided by zero point eight is seven hundred fifty milligrams. The dose exceeds the six hundred milligrams that must be absorbed, because only eighty percent of a capsule reaches the circulation. As a check, five hundred milligrams gave twenty milligrams per liter in part a, so thirty milligrams per liter needs half as much again.",
  "x3-b-adjsata": "What a dosage regimen can adjust. The correct choices are dose size and dosing interval. These are the two parameters a regimen sets. Changing the dose with the interval fixed raises or lowers the steady-state concentrations, and a shorter interval raises them and narrows the fluctuation. Clearance, half-life and volume of distribution belong to the drug in the patient; the regimen is designed around them. Choosing clearance treats a property of the drug in the patient as part of the regimen. Choosing elimination half-life treats the half-life as a dial, but it is set by clearance and volume of distribution, neither of which the regimen changes. She said, So the only things that we can change. Are the dose, so the, the size of the dose, how much drug we give, and the dosing intervals. OK. We don't change clearance, we don't change half-life, we don't change volume and distribution.",
  "x3-b-cavgmean": "The average steady-state concentration over one interval is twenty eight point nine milligrams per liter. The average at steady state is F times D zero divided by the product of V D, k and tau; with F of one, and D zero divided by V D equal to C zero, that is C zero divided by k tau. Each bolus raises the level by C zero, so C zero is the peak minus the trough: fifty three point three minus thirteen point three, forty milligrams per liter. k is zero point one seven three two five per hour and k tau is one point three eight six, so the average is forty milligrams per liter divided by one point three eight six, twenty eight point eight six, rounded to twenty eight point nine milligrams per liter. The common slip is the midpoint, thirty three point three milligrams per liter; the decline is exponential, so the level spends more time near the trough. She said, And I want you to realize that the average is not the max plus the min divided by two. Right? ... because we're looking at logarithmic decline, it is not an, um, algebraic mean, OK?",
  "x3-b-dosesata": "What a larger dose does with the dosing interval unchanged. The correct choices are increased steady-state concentrations and increased fluctuations between peak to trough concentrations. With the interval fixed, a larger dose puts more drug in per interval, so C max, C min and the average at steady state all rise in proportion to the dose. The peak and the trough rise in the same proportion, so the gap between them, which is C zero for a bolus, grows with the dose. Choosing increased patient compliance ties compliance to the dose; the patient takes the same number of doses a day, so compliance usually does not change. Choosing decreased steady-state concentrations reverses the direction. Choosing decreased M T C treats the minimum toxic concentration as part of the regimen. She said, Increasing dose: increase steady-state concentrations; increase fluctuations between peak to trough, maximum and minimum, concentrations; usually no change in patient compliance.",
  "x3-b-end2nd": "C p at the end of the second infusion, in milligrams per liter. The answer is twenty-four point three milligrams per liter. The second infusion's own rise is added to what remains of the first. The infusion rate R is three hundred milligrams divided by two hours, one hundred fifty milligrams per hour, and clearance is zero point one five per hour times fifteen liters, two point two five liters per hour. Each two hour infusion ends at R divided by clearance, sixty-six point six seven, times the quantity one minus e raised to minus the product of k and two hours. That quantity is zero point two five nine two, giving seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter. The first infusion stopped at two hours, so at eight hours it has declined for six hours. Seventeen point two eight times e raised to minus the product of k and six hours, an exponential of zero point four zero six six, leaves seven point zero three milligrams per liter. The sum, twenty-four point three one, rounds to twenty-four point three milligrams per liter. She said, the concentrations from each administration of the dose will be additive over time, OK?",
  "x3-b-intsata": "The effects of lengthening the dosing interval while the dose stays the same. Three options are correct: increased fluctuations between peak and trough concentrations, increased patient compliance, and decreased steady-state concentrations. A longer interval gives the level more time to fall before the next dose, so the trough sits further below the peak and the fluctuation increases. Fewer doses a day are easier for a patient to take as prescribed, so compliance increases. The same dose given less often puts less drug in per hour, so the steady-state C max, C min and average concentration all decrease. Increased steady-state concentrations reverses the direction; steady-state concentrations increase when the interval is shortened, not when it is lengthened. Decreased M T C treats the minimum toxic concentration as part of the regimen; the M T C belongs to the drug and the patient, and the interval moves the concentrations, not the M T C. She said, Increasing dosing interval, decrease steady-state concentrations, increase fluctuations between peak to trough concentrations, increase patient compliance.",
  "cq4-9": "The absolute bioavailability F, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point seven seven. The stem gives an oral A U C, a half-life and a V D but no I V A U C, so the I V area comes from the dose divided by clearance, with clearance equal to k times V D. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by four point five hours, zero point one five four per hour. Clearance is zero point one five four per hour times eighteen liters, two point seven seven two liters per hour. The I V A U C for the same five hundred milligrams is five hundred milligrams divided by two point seven seven two liters per hour, one hundred eighty point four milligrams per liter times hours. The doses are equal, so the dose ratio is one and F is the ratio of the areas: one hundred thirty-nine divided by one hundred eighty point four, which is zero point seven seven one. Two decimal places were asked for, as a decimal, so F is zero point seven seven, not zero point seven seven one.",
  "e2r-3c": "The oral bioavailability F, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point seven five. The stem gives an oral A U C and the total body clearance but no I V area, so the I V area is the dose divided by clearance, and F is the ratio of the areas. The I V A U C for the same five hundred milligrams is five hundred milligrams divided by one point eight six liters per hour, two hundred sixty-eight point eight milligrams per liter times hours. F equals two hundred two divided by two hundred sixty-eight point eight, times five hundred divided by five hundred, which is zero point seven five one five. The same number comes from Cl T times the oral A U C divided by the oral dose: one point eight six times two hundred two, divided by five hundred, is zero point seven five one four. The twelve milligrams per kilogram dose and the V D of fifteen percent of body weight are not needed for F. Rounded, F is zero point seven five, or seventy-five percent, never point seven five, because her rule is no leading decimals.",
  "e2r-3d": "The oral dose that gives the same extent of absorption as the twelve milligrams per kilogram I V dose, in milligrams. The answer is one thousand two hundred milligrams. Same extent of absorption means the same amount of drug reaching the blood, so F times the oral dose equals the I V dose, and the oral dose is the I V dose divided by F. The I V dose is twelve milligrams per kilogram times seventy-five kilograms, nine hundred milligrams. F is zero point seven five from part c. Nine hundred milligrams divided by zero point seven five is one thousand two hundred milligrams. With F unrounded, zero point seven five one four, the dose is one thousand one hundred ninety-seven point seven milligrams, and her solved sheets round an equivalent oral dose to a strength that exists, one thousand two hundred milligrams. The dosing interval of every eight hours is not needed, because extent is per dose. The oral dose is always larger than the nine hundred milligram I V dose, because F is below one.",
  "fr-8a": "The absolute bioavailability of the tablet, as a percent. The answer is seventy percent. F equals the oral A U C divided by the I V A U C, times the I V dose divided by the oral dose. The area ratio is one hundred fifteen divided by one hundred thirty-two, which is zero point eight seven one. The dose ratio is four hundred divided by five hundred, which is zero point eight. Zero point eight seven one times zero point eight is zero point six nine seven, which is seventy percent. The I V A U C goes in the denominator, and the I V dose goes on top of the dose ratio. Eighty-five percent stops at the area ratio; the tablet area came from five hundred milligrams against four hundred milligrams I V, so it has to be scaled by zero point eight to compare equal doses. Ninety-two percent comes from inverting a ratio; with the doses upside down the result is one point zero eight nine, more than all of the dose, but a larger oral dose makes F smaller, not larger. She said, OK, most of you guys thought seventy percent.",
  "fr-8b": "The oral dose that gives similar therapeutic activity as the four hundred milligram bolus, in milligrams, to the nearest five. The answer is five hundred seventy milligrams. Similar activity means the same A U C, the same amount reaching the circulation, so the two A U Cs are set equal and the oral dose is the I V dose divided by F. F is seventy percent from part a. Four hundred milligrams divided by zero point seven zero is five hundred seventy-one point four three milligrams. Only seventy percent of an oral dose reaches the circulation, so the oral dose must be larger than the four hundred milligram I V dose. Five hundred seventy-one point four three is nearer five hundred seventy than five hundred seventy-five. The tablet strength and its A U C are not needed once F is known. With F unrounded, zero point six nine seven, the dose is five hundred seventy-three point eight nine milligrams, which is five hundred seventy-five to the nearest five; both are accepted. She said, Five hundred what? Five hundred seventy? Everybody get five hundred seventy?",
  "m7-c15": "The absolute bioavailability of the tablet, as a percent. The answer is seventy percent. The A U Cs are compared with the I V A U C underneath, then corrected for the doses with the I V dose on top. The area ratio is one hundred fifteen divided by one hundred thirty-two, which is zero point eight seven one. The tablet was the larger dose, so the dose ratio is four hundred divided by five hundred, which is zero point eight. Zero point eight seven one times zero point eight is zero point six nine seven, which is seventy percent. Eighty-five percent stops at the area ratio, which compares the areas but not the doses; the tablet area came from five hundred milligrams against four hundred milligrams I V. Ninety-two percent moves F up for the larger tablet dose instead of down; inverting the dose ratio gives one point zero nine, above one, which an absolute F cannot be. She said, Most of you guys thought seventy percent. OK. OK. Straightforward.",
  "m7-n01": "The absolute bioavailability F of the tablet, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point five five. The tablet is compared with an I V bolus at a different dose, so F equals the oral A U C divided by the I V A U C, times the I V dose divided by the oral dose. Both A U Cs share units and both doses are in milligrams, so the units cancel and F is dimensionless. The area ratio, one hundred one point five divided by seventy-three point eight, is one point three seven five three. The dose ratio, one hundred milligrams divided by two hundred fifty milligrams, is zero point four. Their product is zero point five five zero one, which is zero point five five. The tablet's A U C is larger than the I V A U C only because its dose was two and a half times larger. Without the dose ratio the answer would be one point three eight, an absolute F above one, which is impossible. The six volunteers are not needed, because the A U Cs are already averages. She said, And I'm hearing zero point five five.",
  "m7-n04": "The absolute bioavailability F of the oral tablet, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point eight one. No I V area is given, so the I V A U C is built from the I V data: the half-life gives k, k times V D is the clearance, and the I V dose divided by the clearance is the I V A U C. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by three hours, zero point two three one per hour. Clearance is zero point two three one per hour times twenty-five liters, five point seven seven five liters per hour. The I V A U C is five hundred milligrams divided by five point seven seven five liters per hour, eighty-six point five eight milligrams per liter times hours. Both doses were five hundred milligrams, so the dose ratio is one and F is the area ratio alone: seventy divided by eighty-six point five eight, which is zero point eight zero eight five. Rounded, F is zero point eight one, about eighty-one percent. She said, So I'm getting about eighty-one percent. And recognize that F is a dimensionless number, right?",
  "ws7-1a": "The bioavailability F of the tablet, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point six six seven. The I V curve is given as an equation, so its intercept and slope give V D and clearance, and clearance times the oral A U C is the amount absorbed, F times the oral dose. One microgram per milliliter is one milligram per liter, so the oral area is four hundred thirty-five milligrams per liter times hours. V D is the I V dose divided by the intercept: two hundred fifty milligrams divided by thirty milligrams per liter, eight point three three liters. F equals the oral A U C times k times V D, divided by the oral dose. The oral area times zero point zero nine two per hour times eight point three three liters is three hundred thirty-three point four milligrams, and divided by five hundred milligrams that is zero point six six seven. The two-A U C form gives the same value, with an I V A U C of thirty milligrams per liter divided by zero point zero nine two per hour, which is three hundred twenty-six milligrams per liter times hours.",
  "ws7-3a": "The bioavailability F of the oral capsule, as a decimal fraction. The answer is zero point six six seven. No I V area is given, so clearance comes from the half-life and the volume, and clearance times the oral A U C is the amount that reached the circulation, F times the oral dose. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by four point six hours, zero point one five zero seven per hour. Cl T is eighteen liters times zero point one five zero seven per hour, two point seven one two liters per hour. Two point seven one two liters per hour times two hundred forty-six milligram hours per liter leaves six hundred sixty-seven milligrams, the amount that reached the circulation. Six hundred sixty-seven milligrams divided by one thousand milligrams is zero point six six seven, two thirds of the capsule dose. F lies between zero and one; a value above one means the dose and the absorbed amount were swapped.",
  "cq5-1": "The effect of a longer elimination half-life on the duration of activity. The answer is that the duration of activity will increase. A longer half-life means slower elimination, so the level takes longer to fall from C zero to the minimum effective concentration. In the duration equation, the duration t eff equals one point four four times the half-life times the natural log of a ratio: D zero divided by the product of V D and C eff, the minimum effective concentration, so the half-life multiplies the whole expression. The duration of activity therefore changes in direct proportion to the half-life: twice the half-life, twice the duration. Decreased renal or hepatic function prolongs the half-life, prolongs the effect, and raises the possibility of toxicity. Choosing a decrease reverses the direction; a longer half-life means a smaller elimination rate constant k, so the level stays at or above the minimum effective concentration for longer, not shorter. Choosing no change treats the duration as set by the dose alone.",
  "cq5-2": "The result of doubling an I V bolus dose in linear, first-order pharmacokinetics. The answer is that the duration of action is increased by one half-life. Doubling D zero doubles C zero, and the level needs exactly one half-life to fall from two C zero back to C zero. The duration is the difference between the natural log of C zero and the natural log of C eff, the minimum effective concentration, divided by k, so doubling the dose adds the natural log of two divided by k, which is zero point six nine three divided by k, one half-life. Choosing a doubled duration treats the duration as proportional to the dose, but the dose sits inside a logarithm. Choosing a half-life that halves or doubles treats the half-life as dose-dependent; in linear, first-order kinetics k does not change with the dose, and a half-life that changes with dose marks a saturable, nonlinear drug. For an antibiotic with a V D of twenty-four liters and a k of zero point one five per hour, two hundred fifty milligrams lasts twenty-six point three five hours and five hundred milligrams lasts thirty point nine seven hours, four point six two hours more.",
  "cq5-3": "The duration of action in hours, to one decimal place. The answer is twelve point four hours. One I V bolus with the half-life, V D and minimum effective concentration given calls for the natural-log duration line: the difference between the natural log of C zero and the natural log of C eff, the minimum effective concentration, divided by k. Six micrograms per milliliter equals six milligrams per liter, so no conversion factor is needed. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by five hours, zero point one three eight six per hour. C zero is six hundred four milligrams divided by eighteen liters, thirty-three point five six milligrams per liter. The ratio of C zero to C eff is five point five nine three, and its natural log, one point seven two one, divided by zero point one three eight six per hour gives twelve point four two hours, which rounds to twelve point four. Using base-ten logs without the two point three gives five point three nine hours, far too short. The ratio lies between four and eight, so the duration lies between two and three half-lives.",
  "cq5-4": "The single I V bolus dose that gives a twenty hour duration of action, in milligrams. The answer is eight hundred eighteen milligrams. The level must reach C eff, the minimum effective concentration, at t eff, twenty hours after the dose, so C equals C zero times e to the minus k t is solved for the dose: D zero equals C eff times V D times e raised to the product of k and t eff. V D is zero point two five liters per kilogram times seventy-five kilograms, eighteen point seven five liters. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by five point eight hours, zero point one one nine five per hour. C eff times V D is four milligrams per liter times eighteen point seven five liters, seventy-five milligrams, the amount in the body at C eff. k times t eff is two point three nine zero, and e to that power is ten point nine one. Seventy-five milligrams times ten point nine one is eight hundred eighteen point two milligrams, which rounds to eight hundred eighteen. Dividing by the exponential instead gives six point nine milligrams, far too small.",
  "fr-11a": "The duration of action of a one gram I V bolus. The answer is eight hours. C zero is one thousand milligrams divided by twelve point five liters, eighty milligrams per liter, four times the minimum effective concentration. The level halves every four hours, so eighty to forty to twenty milligrams per liter takes two half-lives, eight hours. Four hours counts one half-life, eighty to forty milligrams per liter, and stops above the minimum effective concentration; four hours is the duration of a five hundred milligram dose. Twelve hours counts one half-life too many; three half-lives take the level to ten milligrams per liter, half the minimum effective concentration. None of the above usually follows leaving the dose in grams; one gram has to be one thousand milligrams before it is divided by twelve point five liters. She said, And most of you guys thought that it was eight hours, and that is the correct answer.",
  "fr-11b": "The duration of action of a five hundred milligram I V bolus, in hours. The answer is four hours. A single I V bolus with V D, half-life and minimum effective concentration given calls for the natural-log duration line, with C zero equal to the dose divided by V D. C zero is five hundred milligrams divided by twelve point five liters, forty milligrams per liter. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by four hours, zero point one seven three two five per hour. The duration is the natural log of the ratio of forty to twenty, which is zero point six nine three one, divided by zero point one seven three two five per hour, which is four point zero zero hours. Forty milligrams per liter is twice the minimum effective concentration of twenty milligrams per liter, so the level reaches it after one half-life. Halving the dose takes one half-life, four hours, off the eight hours of part a. Half the dose does not give half the duration in general, because the dose sits inside the logarithm; here it does only because eight hours is two half-lives. She said, Four hours, right?",
  "hw7-1b": "The duration of activity, in hours. The answer is seventeen point nine three hours. Duration of activity with a minimum effective concentration calls for the natural-log duration line, with C zero equal to D zero divided by V D and k from the half-life. The dose and the volume are both per kilogram, so the kilograms cancel: twelve milligrams per kilogram divided by zero point two five liters per kilogram is forty-eight milligrams per liter. Eight hundred forty milligrams over seventeen point five liters gives the same forty-eight milligrams per liter. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by five hours, zero point one three eight six per hour. The duration is the natural log of forty-eight divided by four, the natural log of twelve, which is two point four eight five, divided by zero point one three eight six per hour, which is seventeen point nine three hours. The twelve-fold fall lies between eight and sixteen, so the duration lies between three and four half-lives of five hours.",
  "hw7-1c": "The duration of activity for a six milligrams per kilogram dose, in hours. The answer is twelve point nine three hours. The same natural-log duration line applies with the new C zero; k and the minimum effective concentration do not change. Half the dose gives half of C zero, because the volume does not change with the dose: six milligrams per kilogram divided by zero point two five liters per kilogram is twenty-four milligrams per liter. The elimination rate constant k is zero point one three eight six per hour, and C eff is four milligrams per liter. The duration is the natural log of twenty-four divided by four, the natural log of six, which is one point seven nine two, divided by zero point one three eight six per hour, which is twelve point nine three hours. Halving the dose removes one doubling, and each doubling adds one half-life, so five hours comes off: seventeen point nine three minus twelve point nine three is five hours. The seventeen point nine three hours is not halved.",
  "hw7-1d": "The dose that gives a ten hour duration of action, in milligrams. The answer is two hundred seventy-nine point nine milligrams. C equals C zero times e to the minus k t, written at t eff, the moment the level reaches C eff, the minimum effective concentration, and solved for the dose, gives D zero equals C eff times V D times e raised to the product of k and t eff. V D is zero point two five liters per kilogram times seventy kilograms, seventeen point five liters. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by five hours, zero point one three eight six per hour. C eff times V D is four milligrams per liter times seventeen point five liters, seventy milligrams, the amount in the body at C eff. k times t eff is one point three eight six, and e to that power is three point nine nine nine. Seventy milligrams times three point nine nine nine is two hundred seventy-nine point nine milligrams, or four point zero milligrams per kilogram. Ten hours is two half-lives of five hours, so the dose is four times the seventy milligrams.",
  "pd-c13": "Whether doubling the I V bolus dose doubles the duration of activity. The answer is false. Doubling D zero doubles C zero, and the level needs exactly one half-life to fall from two C zero back to C zero; after that it follows the old curve. So the duration of activity grows by one half-life, not two-fold. Twenty-six point three five hours becomes thirty point nine seven hours when two hundred fifty milligrams is doubled to five hundred milligrams, with a V D of twenty-four liters and a k of zero point one five per hour. Choosing true treats duration as proportional to dose. The dose sits inside a logarithm: the duration t eff equals one point four four times the half-life times the natural log of D zero divided by the product of V D and C eff, the minimum effective concentration. So doubling the dose adds the natural log of two divided by k, one half-life, instead of doubling the duration.",
  "pd-c14": "The change in the duration of activity, t eff, when a single I V bolus dose is doubled. The answer is that t eff rises by one half-life. The duration is the difference between the natural log of C zero and the natural log of C eff, the minimum effective concentration, divided by k. Doubling D zero doubles C zero and adds the natural log of two to the top, so t eff rises by zero point six nine three divided by k, exactly one half-life. Each further doubling of the dose adds one more half-life. Choosing t eff doubles treats the duration as proportional to D zero; D zero is inside the logarithm, so two D zero adds one half-life rather than doubling t eff. Choosing two half-lives takes a factor of four for a factor of two; quadrupling the dose, two doublings, adds two half-lives. Choosing no change treats duration as a fixed property of the drug, like the half-life; the half-life does not change with dose, but a higher C zero takes longer to fall to C eff.",
  "pd-ex-a": "The duration of activity, t eff, for a single two hundred fifty milligram I V dose, in hours. The answer is twenty-six point three five hours. The stem gives a single I V dose, a minimum effective concentration and a first-order elimination rate constant, so the natural-log duration line applies, and it takes k as given. Zero point two micrograms per milliliter equals zero point two milligrams per liter, because one microgram per milliliter equals one milligram per liter. C zero is two hundred fifty milligrams divided by twenty-four liters, ten point four two milligrams per liter. The ratio ten point four two divided by zero point two is fifty-two point zero eight, and its natural log, three point nine five three, divided by zero point one five per hour gives twenty-six point three five hours. The half-life is zero point six nine three divided by zero point one five per hour, which is four point six two hours, and the ratio lies between two to the fifth and two to the sixth, so the duration is between five and six half-lives.",
  "pd-ex-c": "The new duration of activity, t eff, at five hundred milligrams, in hours. The answer is thirty point nine seven hours. The natural-log duration line applies with the doubled C zero, and k is given. Twice the dose gives twice C zero, because V D does not change with the dose: five hundred milligrams divided by twenty-four liters is twenty point eight three milligrams per liter. C eff is zero point two micrograms per milliliter, which is zero point two milligrams per liter. The ratio twenty point eight three divided by zero point two is one hundred four point two, and its natural log, four point six four six, divided by zero point one five per hour gives thirty point nine seven hours. The logarithm grew by the natural log of two, which is zero point six nine three, and zero point six nine three divided by zero point one five per hour is the half-life, four point six two hours. Thirty point nine seven minus twenty-six point three five is four point six two hours, one half-life. The duration is not fifty-two point seven hours, because the dose sits inside the logarithm.",
  "pd-ex-d": "The dose that doubles t eff, in grams. The answer is thirteen grams. Doubling the duration means the level must still be at C eff, the minimum effective concentration, fifty-two point seven hours after the dose, twice twenty-six point three five hours. C equals C zero times e to the minus k t, solved for the dose at that moment, gives D zero equals C eff times V D times e raised to the product of k and t eff. C eff is zero point two milligrams per liter, and times twenty-four liters that is four point eight milligrams. The exponent is zero point one five per hour times fifty-two point seven hours, seven point nine zero five, with no units, and e to that power is two thousand seven hundred ten point eight. Multiplying gives thirteen thousand twelve milligrams, rounded to thirteen grams. That is about fifty-two times the two hundred fifty milligram dose, because the extra twenty-six point three five hours is five point seven more half-lives, and each half-life of duration needs the dose doubled once. Doubling the dose adds only one half-life, so doubling the duration needs far more than twice two hundred fifty milligrams.",
  "pd-ic1a": "The duration of action of the two hundred fifty milligram bolus, in hours. The answer is nine point one six hours. The half-life is given, not k, so the half-life form of the duration line applies: t eff equals one point four four times the half-life times the natural log of D zero divided by the product of V D and C eff, the minimum effective concentration. One point two micrograms per milliliter equals one point two milligrams per liter. V D times C eff is twenty-five liters times one point two milligrams per liter, thirty milligrams, the amount in the body at C eff. The dose is eight point three three three times that, and the natural log of that ratio is two point one two zero. Multiplying one point four four by three hours gives four point three two hours, and times two point one two zero that is nine point one six hours. With k equal to zero point two three one per hour the result is nine point one eight hours, and her working gives nine point one five hours; the one point four four is rounded, so the forms differ only in the second decimal.",
  "pd-ic1b": "The dose that gives a twelve hour duration of action, in milligrams. The answer is four hundred seventy-nine point seven milligrams. The dose line has k in its exponent, so k comes first: zero point six nine three divided by three hours, zero point two three one per hour. C eff, the minimum effective concentration, is one point two micrograms per milliliter, or one point two milligrams per liter. C equals C zero times e to the minus k t, written at t eff, the target duration, when the level reaches C eff, and solved for the dose, gives D zero equals C eff times V D times e raised to the product of k and t eff. C eff times V D is one point two milligrams per liter times twenty-five liters, thirty milligrams, the amount in the body at that level. The exponent is two point seven seven two, and e to that power is fifteen point nine nine. Thirty milligrams times fifteen point nine nine is four hundred seventy-nine point seven milligrams. Twelve hours is four half-lives of three hours, and two to the fourth is sixteen, so the dose is about sixteen times the thirty milligrams.",
  "pd-p1a": "The duration of activity, t eff, of a single five hundred milligram I V bolus, in hours. The answer is eight point six nine hours. Her key uses the k form of the natural-log duration line, so the five hour half-life is turned into k first: zero point six nine three divided by five hours is zero point one three eight six per hour. C eff is ten micrograms per milliliter, which equals ten milligrams per liter. C zero is the dose spread through V D: five hundred milligrams divided by fifteen liters, thirty-three point three three milligrams per liter. The level only has to fall from thirty-three point three three to ten milligrams per liter, a three point three three fold fall, before the effect ends. The natural log of three point three three three is one point two zero four, and one point two zero four divided by zero point one three eight six per hour is eight point six nine hours. The ratio lies between two and four, so the duration lies between one and two half-lives of five hours.",
  "pd-p2a": "The expected duration of activity, t eff, in hours. The answer is seven hours. The dose and the volume are both per kilogram, so the weight is converted first: one hundred sixty-five pounds divided by two point two is seventy-five kilograms. D zero is five milligrams per kilogram times seventy-five kilograms, three hundred seventy-five milligrams, and V D is zero point one five liters per kilogram times seventy-five kilograms, eleven point two five liters. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by four point seven five hours, zero point one four five nine per hour. C eff is twelve micrograms per milliliter, which equals twelve milligrams per liter. C zero is three hundred seventy-five milligrams divided by eleven point two five liters, thirty-three point three three milligrams per liter. The ratio thirty-three point three three divided by twelve is two point seven seven eight, and its natural log is one point zero two two. Dividing by zero point one four five nine per hour gives seven point zero zero hours. The ratio lies between two and four, so the duration lies between one and two half-lives.",
  "pd-p2b": "The dose per kilogram for a twelve hour duration of activity. The answer is ten point four milligrams per kilogram. One hundred sixty-five pounds divided by two point two is seventy-five kilograms, so V D is zero point one five liters per kilogram times seventy-five kilograms, eleven point two five liters. The elimination rate constant k is zero point six nine three divided by four point seven five hours, zero point one four five nine per hour. For the level to be at C eff, the minimum effective concentration of twelve milligrams per liter, at t eff, twelve hours, C zero must be higher by the factor e raised to the product of k and t eff, which is five point seven five nine. Twelve milligrams per liter times five point seven five nine is sixty-nine point one one milligrams per liter. The dose is C zero times V D, seven hundred seventy-seven point five milligrams, and divided by seventy-five kilograms that is ten point four milligrams per kilogram. Twelve hours is about one half-life longer than the seven hours of five milligrams per kilogram, so the dose roughly doubles.",
  "pd-p3a": "The expected duration of action, in hours. The answer is eleven point five six hours. The half-life is given, so the half-life form of the duration line applies, with C zero equal to D zero divided by V D. The dose and the volume are both per kilogram, so the kilograms cancel and the seventy-five kilogram weight is not needed: twenty milligrams per kilogram divided by zero point three liters per kilogram is sixty-six point six seven milligrams per liter. C eff is seven point two micrograms per milliliter, which equals seven point two milligrams per liter. The level must fall nine point two five nine fold, and the natural log of nine point two five nine is two point two two six. Her key writes the half-life form as the half-life over zero point six nine three, so three point six hours times two point two two six, which is eight point zero one three, is divided by zero point six nine three, giving eleven point five six hours. The ratio lies between eight and sixteen, so the duration lies between three and four half-lives of three point six hours.",
  "pd-p3b": "The duration of action when the dose is doubled, in hours. The answer is fifteen point one six hours. The same half-life form of the duration line applies with C zero doubled; the half-life and the minimum effective concentration do not change. The doubled dose is forty milligrams per kilogram, and divided by zero point three liters per kilogram it gives a C zero of one hundred thirty-three point three milligrams per liter, twice the earlier C zero, because the volume does not change with the dose. The ratio one hundred thirty-three point three divided by seven point two is eighteen point five two, and its natural log is two point nine one nine. Multiplying three point six hours by two point nine one nine and dividing by zero point six nine three gives fifteen point one six hours. Fifteen point one six minus eleven point five six is three point six hours, one half-life, which her key states as an increase of one half-life. Doubling the dose adds one half-life to the duration; it does not double eleven point five six hours.",
  "x3-c-07": "The duration of action of a two gram I V bolus. The answer is twelve hours. C zero is two thousand milligrams divided by twelve point five liters, one hundred sixty milligrams per liter, eight times the minimum effective concentration. Eight is two times two times two, so the level falls from one hundred sixty to eighty to forty to twenty milligrams per liter in three half-lives of four hours, twelve hours. Eight hours keeps the duration of a one gram dose; twice that dose needs one more half-life, four hours, to fall to twenty milligrams per liter. Sixteen hours doubles the eight hour duration along with the dose; the dose sits inside a logarithm, so doubling it adds one half-life to the eight hours instead. With these values, five hundred milligrams lasts four hours, one gram lasts eight hours, and two grams last twelve hours. From five hundred milligrams to one gram the duration happens to double, because four hours is one half-life; from one gram to two grams it does not.",
  "x3-c-08": "The effects of doubling an I V bolus dose in linear kinetics. Three options are correct: the A U C doubles, the initial plasma concentration doubles, and the duration of action increases by one half-life. The A U C equals D zero divided by clearance, and clearance does not change with the dose, so twice the dose gives twice the area. C zero equals D zero divided by V D, and V D does not change with the dose, so twice the dose gives twice the initial concentration. Doubling C zero adds the natural log of two to the natural log of C zero, and the natural log of two divided by k is one half-life. The duration of action doubling is wrong, because the dose sits inside the logarithm. The half-life doubling is wrong, because k does not depend on the dose; a half-life that changes with dose marks a nonlinear drug. The clearance doubling is wrong, because clearance belongs to the drug and the patient. She said, Because we expect for our linear pharmacokinetics, a proportional increase, decrease, or whatever, change. In the A U C as well as the concentration as we change the dose.",
  "x3-c-09": "Why doubling the dose doubled the duration of action here. The answer is that the four hour duration equals one half-life. Doubling the dose always adds one half-life, here four hours. The five hundred milligram dose gives a C zero of forty milligrams per liter, twice the minimum effective concentration, so its duration is exactly one half-life, and adding one half-life doubles it. From any other starting duration, adding one half-life does not double it. The option saying duration of action is proportional to the dose generalises from one pair of doses; with two grams the duration is twelve hours, not sixteen, because the dose sits inside a logarithm. The option saying the half-life doubles when the dose doubles moves the half-life with the dose; for linear, first-order kinetics the half-life stays four hours at every dose, and it is C zero that doubles. She said, Everybody know what happens if we double the dose? You get one half-life.",
  "x3-c-10": "The duration of action after the dose is increased by a factor of four, to six thousand milligrams, in hours. The answer is eighteen point seven six hours. In the half-life form of the duration line, V D, the minimum effective concentration and the half-life do not change with the dose, so only the logarithm changes, by the natural log of four. A factor of four is two doublings, and each doubling adds one half-life, because the natural log of four is twice the natural log of two, and the natural log of two divided by k is one half-life. The added duration is two times three point six hours, seven point two hours. The new duration is eleven point five six hours plus seven point two hours, eighteen point seven six hours. The volume and the minimum effective concentration are not needed for the change. Eighteen point seven six hours is far short of four times eleven point five six hours. For each doubling of the dose, she said, Increase of one half-life, three point six hours.",
  "x3-c-12": "The dose for an eighteen hour duration, t eff, in milligrams. The answer is five hundred fifty-nine point eight milligrams. One hundred fifty-four pounds divided by two point two is seventy kilograms, so V D is zero point two five liters per kilogram times seventy kilograms, seventeen point five liters. C eff, the minimum effective concentration, is four micrograms per milliliter, or four milligrams per liter. Solving C equals C zero times e to the minus k t for the dose at t eff gives D zero equals C eff times V D times e raised to the product of k and t eff. k is zero point six nine three divided by six hours, zero point one one five five per hour. C eff times V D is seventy milligrams, the amount left at C eff. The exponent is two point zero seven nine; e to that power is seven point nine nine six. Seventy milligrams times seven point nine nine six is five hundred fifty-nine point eight milligrams, eight times seventy milligrams, because eighteen hours is three half-lives. Leaving the weight in pounds gives a volume of thirty-eight point five liters and more than twice this dose.",
  "cq5-5": "Which statement about nonlinear elimination is true. The answer is that at low plasma concentrations relative to K M, the elimination rate depends on the concentration. The rate of elimination is V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p. With C p much less than K M, C p is negligible in K M plus C p, and the rate becomes V max divided by K M, times C p. V max and K M are constants, so the rate is proportional to C p, which is first-order behaviour. The option saying the rate is independent of concentration at low C p relative to K M reverses the two ends; a rate independent of concentration is zero order, which holds when C p is much greater than K M and the enzyme runs at V max. The options comparing C p with V max use the wrong parameter; V max is a rate, an amount per time, so a concentration cannot be low or high relative to it. K M and C p share units of concentration, which is why they are the pair compared.",
  "cq5-6": "Which statement about nonlinear pharmacokinetics is true. The answer is that the half-life changes as the dose is increased. With saturable elimination, the half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p. So a larger dose, giving a higher C p, gives a longer half-life, one of the characteristics of saturation kinetics. Clearance equals V max divided by the sum of K M and C p, so clearance falls as C p rises. The option saying parameters do not change with different or multiple doses is the definition of linear pharmacokinetics. The option saying superposition applies at steady state carries a linear rule into saturation kinetics; once elimination saturates, a later dose is cleared more slowly, so the curves do not simply add. The option saying the A U C is proportional to the amount of bioavailable drug describes dose-independent kinetics; with saturable elimination the A U C is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug.",
  "cq5-7": "The daily dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred nine milligrams per day. A target steady-state concentration with K M and V max given calls for the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. V max, six hundred twenty-five milligrams per day, is already a daily amount, so no weight is needed. Six hundred twenty-five milligrams per day times fifteen milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of seven point nine and fifteen, which is twenty-two point nine milligrams per liter, is four hundred nine point four milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. Her key gives four hundred nine; the nearest five is four hundred ten, and both score. The common slip is leaving K M out of the bottom, which gives V max itself, six hundred twenty-five milligrams per day, far too high.",
  "cq5-8": "The Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is five point four milligrams per liter. Two daily doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The seventy-five kilogram weight and the age are set aside, because the doses stay in milligrams per day. The top is four hundred fifty minus three hundred fifty, which is one hundred milligrams per day. Three hundred fifty divided by eight point seven milligrams per liter is forty point two three, and four hundred fifty divided by twenty point six milligrams per liter is twenty-one point eight four, so the bottom is eighteen point three nine milligrams per day per milligram per liter. One hundred divided by eighteen point three nine is five point four three eight milligrams per liter, which rounds to five point four. Pairing each dose with the other dose's concentration makes the bottom negative and gives a negative K M, which cannot be right.",
  "fr-9": "Which kinetic order Michaelis-Menten elimination shows at low and at high concentrations. The answer is False, because the statement has the orders the wrong way round. The elimination rate equals V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p. Well below K M, the rate is V max divided by K M, times C p, so the rate is proportional to the concentration, which is first order. Well above K M, the rate is V max whatever the concentration, which is zero order. Low dose, first order; high dose, zero order. V max and K M are constants for a given drug in a given patient. Picking True reverses the two ends. She said, At high concentrations, then the process proceeds according to VM max, right? V max is a constant for a given drug given patient.",
  "nl-c01": "Which kinetic order a saturable drug shows at each end of the concentration range. The answer is False: the statement has the orders the other way round. In Michaelis-Menten kinetics the elimination rate equals V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p. When C p is much greater than K M, K M is negligible, the rate equals V max, and the rate does not depend on concentration, which is zero order. When C p is much less than K M, the rate is V max divided by K M, times C p, which is k prime times C p, so the rate is proportional to concentration, which is first order. Choosing True reverses the two ends: first order belongs at low concentrations and zero order at high ones.",
  "nl-c02": "The difference between linear and nonlinear pharmacokinetics. The answer is that linear parameters stay fixed with dose, while nonlinear ones change with dose. In linear pharmacokinetics, parameters such as half-life and clearance do not change when different or multiple doses are given, and most drugs follow this. In nonlinear pharmacokinetics, also called saturable, dose-dependent or capacity-limited, a larger dose or chronic administration changes those parameters, so a small dose change can give a large concentration change. Phenytoin is the example. The tempting wrong answer, linear is zero order and nonlinear is first order, reverses the labels: linear is the first-order elimination of most drugs, and a saturable drug looks first order at low concentrations and zero order at high ones. The option saying the nonlinear A U C rises in direct proportion to the dose attaches the linear property to the wrong model: proportional A U C is curve C, dose-independent kinetics.",
  "nl-c03": "How half-life and total clearance move as the concentration of a nonlinear drug rises. The answer is that the half-life increases and Cl T decreases. Half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p, so the half-life grows as C p grows. Cl T equals V max divided by the sum of K M and C p, and C p sits in the denominator, so clearance shrinks as C p grows. In one phenytoin patient, going from fifteen to twenty-one milligrams per liter took the half-life from one point eight seven to two point three four days, and clearance from twenty point eight to sixteen point seven liters per day. The option where the half-life decreases and clearance increases runs both lines the opposite direction. The option where neither changes treats the half-life and clearance as constants because V max and K M are constants, but both lines also contain C p, which changes with dose.",
  "nl-c04": "The characteristics of saturation kinetics. Four options are correct. First, the elimination half-life changes as dose is increased, because half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p, so a higher dose, giving a higher C p, gives a longer half-life. Second, A U C is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug: once elimination saturates, a larger dose is cleared more slowly, so the area under the curve rises more than in proportion to the dose. Third, other drugs using the same enzyme or carrier can affect saturation, because two drugs that need the same enzyme or carrier compete for its limited capacity. Fourth, metabolite ratios may change with dose, because one metabolic route can saturate while others do not. The option saying clearance stays constant carries the linear rule into saturation kinetics: Cl T equals V max divided by the sum of K M and C p, and falls as C p rises. The option saying A U C rises in direct proportion at every dose describes curve C, dose-independent kinetics.",
  "nl-c08": "The rate of elimination of a saturable drug when C p is much greater than K M. The answer is that the rate equals V max, so elimination is zero order. The rate equals V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p. With C p much greater than K M, K M is negligible in the sum of K M and C p, the C p terms cancel, and the rate equals V max, a constant. A rate independent of concentration is zero order: more drug is not removed any faster, so further dose increases accumulate in the body. The option V max divided by K M, times C p, first order, applies the low-concentration limit at the wrong end; here it is K M that drops out. The option that the rate falls to zero reads saturated as stopped: a saturated enzyme works at its maximum, V max, not at zero.",
  "nl-c09": "The rate of elimination of a saturable drug when C p is much less than K M. The answer is that the rate equals V max divided by K M, times C p, so elimination looks first order. In the rate, V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p, the C p in the sum is negligible next to K M. That leaves V max divided by K M, times C p. V max and K M are both constants, so their ratio is a constant, k prime, and a rate proportional to C p is first order. The option that the rate equals V max, zero order, applies the high-concentration limit at the wrong end; the rate equals V max only when C p is much greater than K M. The option that the rate is zero reads a low concentration as no elimination; a low concentration gives a proportionally low rate.",
  "nl-c18": "Whether clearance and half-life stay constant in nonlinear pharmacokinetics. The answer is True: they are usually not constant. Cl T equals V max divided by the sum of K M and C p, and the half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p. Both lines contain C p, so both change as the dose, and with it the concentration, changes. Saturable, or dose-dependent, pharmacokinetics shows nonlinear changes in plasma concentrations and A U C with increases in dose or multiple administrations. In linear pharmacokinetics clearance and half-life are both fixed. Choosing False carries the linear rule into nonlinear kinetics: a fixed half-life and clearance hold for first-order drugs, but with saturable elimination both depend on the concentration.",
  "nl-ex1": "The daily dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred thirty milligrams per day. K M, V max and a target average concentration point to the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s, because at steady state the dose rate equals the rate of saturable elimination. The C s s is the twenty-four milligrams per liter average. Eight milligrams per kilogram per day times twenty-four milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of five and twenty-four milligrams per liter, is one hundred ninety-two divided by twenty-nine, or six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day; milligrams per liter cancel, so R keeps the units of V max. That rate is per kilogram, so it is multiplied by the sixty-five kilogram weight: four hundred thirty point three milligrams per day, which rounds to four hundred thirty. Stopping at the per-kilogram rate leaves a value that is not yet a dose for this patient.",
  "nl-ex2a": "The Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is ten milligrams per liter. Six milligrams per kilogram per day gave ten milligrams per liter, and eight milligrams per kilogram per day gave twenty milligrams per liter. Two rates and two steady states in one patient call for the direct method. K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The seventy-two kilogram weight is not needed. The top is eight minus six, or two milligrams per kilogram per day. Six divided by ten is zero point six, and eight divided by twenty is zero point four, so the bottom is zero point two milligrams per kilogram per day per milligram per liter. Dividing two by zero point two gives ten milligrams per liter, because the milligrams per kilogram per day cancel and milligrams per liter move to the top. A K M in milligrams per kilogram per day signals the units were not cancelled.",
  "nl-ex2b": "The maximum rate of saturable elimination, V max, in milligrams per kilogram per day. The answer is twelve milligrams per kilogram per day. With K M known from part a as ten milligrams per liter, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s. Any one dose rate with its own steady-state concentration will do. With the second pair, eight milligrams per kilogram per day and twenty milligrams per liter, eight times the sum of ten and twenty gives two hundred forty, and two hundred forty divided by twenty is twelve milligrams per kilogram per day. Milligrams per liter cancel, so V max keeps the per-kilogram units of the doses. The first pair, six milligrams per kilogram per day with ten milligrams per liter, gives the same twelve. Mixing one pair's R with the other pair's C s s gives a wrong value. V max must exceed both dose rates, and twelve is above eight.",
  "nl-ex2c": "The daily dose rate R for this patient, in milligrams per day. The answer is five hundred eighteen point four milligrams per day. The target of fifteen milligrams per liter goes into the nonlinear dose-rate line, R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s, with K M of ten milligrams per liter from part a and V max of twelve milligrams per kilogram per day from part b. Twelve times fifteen is one hundred eighty, and ten plus fifteen is twenty-five milligrams per liter, so R is seven point two milligrams per kilogram per day, per kilogram because V max is. That rate sits between the six and eight milligrams per kilogram per day rates, as fifteen sits between ten and twenty milligrams per liter. The seventy-two kilogram weight is used only here: seven point two milligrams per kilogram per day times seventy-two kilograms is five hundred eighteen point four milligrams per day. Her worked answer rounds it to about five hundred twenty milligrams per day, and both are accepted.",
  "nl-hw-a": "This patient's Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is five point two one milligrams per liter. Two daily doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The seventy-two kilogram weight is not needed. The top is three hundred fifty minus two hundred fifty, which is one hundred milligrams per day. Two hundred fifty divided by six point four milligrams per liter is thirty-nine point zero six three, and three hundred fifty divided by seventeen point six is nineteen point eight eight six, leaving nineteen point one seven seven milligrams per day per milligram per liter on the bottom. One hundred divided by nineteen point one seven seven is five point two one five milligrams per liter, reported as five point two one. The two ratios are close, so rounding them early moves K M noticeably.",
  "nl-hw-b": "The maximum elimination rate V max in this patient, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred fifty-three point seven milligrams per day. With K M from part a, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s, using one dose and the steady-state concentration it produced. Three hundred fifty milligrams per day times the sum of five point two one five and seventeen point six milligrams per liter gives seven thousand nine hundred eighty-five point two five, and dividing by seventeen point six milligrams per liter gives four hundred fifty-three point seven milligrams per day. Milligrams per liter cancel, so V max keeps the units of the dose. The two hundred fifty milligrams per day pair with six point four milligrams per liter gives the same value. V max must exceed the three hundred fifty milligrams per day dose that reached a steady state.",
  "nl-hw-c": "The daily phenytoin dose rate R for a target of fourteen milligrams per liter, in milligrams per day. The answer is three hundred thirty milligrams per day. The patient's own K M, five point two one five milligrams per liter from part a, and V max, four hundred fifty-three point seven milligrams per day from part b, go into the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. Four hundred fifty-three point seven times fourteen is six thousand three hundred fifty-one point eight, and five point two one five plus fourteen is nineteen point two one five milligrams per liter. Dividing gives three hundred thirty point six milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. The stem asks for the nearest ten, so the answer is three hundred thirty milligrams per day. Fourteen milligrams per liter lies between six point four and seventeen point six, so the dose must lie between two hundred fifty and three hundred fifty milligrams per day, and it does.",
  "nl-ic1": "The daily dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred milligrams per day. A target steady-state concentration with K M and V max given calls for the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. V max, five hundred eighty-four milligrams per day, is already a daily amount, so no weight is needed. Five hundred eighty-four milligrams per day times fifteen milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of six point nine and fifteen, which is twenty-one point nine milligrams per liter, is four hundred point zero milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. The target C s s appears on top and again, added to K M, on the bottom, which keeps R below V max for any target. C s s is about two-thirds of the sum of K M and C s s, so R is about two-thirds of five hundred eighty-four, near four hundred milligrams per day.",
  "nl-ic2": "The Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is six point four three milligrams per liter. Two daily doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The seventy-five kilogram weight is set aside, because the doses stay in milligrams per day. The top is four hundred fifty minus three hundred fifty, which is one hundred milligrams per day. Three hundred fifty divided by nine point two milligrams per liter is thirty-eight point zero four, and four hundred fifty divided by twenty is twenty-two point five, so the bottom is fifteen point five four milligrams per day per milligram per liter. One hundred divided by fifteen point five four is six point four three milligrams per liter. Dividing the doses by seventy-five kilograms and rounding four point six seven to four point six milligrams per kilogram per day gives seven point zero milligrams per liter, a rounding error.",
  "nl-ic3": "The patient's maximum elimination rate V max, in milligrams per day. The answer is five hundred ninety-five milligrams per day. With K M of six point four three milligrams per liter from the previous question, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s. Either dose with its own steady-state concentration can be used. With four hundred fifty milligrams per day and its own twenty milligrams per liter, four hundred fifty times the sum of six point four three and twenty, divided by twenty, is five hundred ninety-four point seven milligrams per day; milligrams per liter cancel. The first pair, three hundred fifty milligrams per day with nine point two milligrams per liter, gives five hundred ninety-four point six, the same within rounding, because both doses were given to one patient with one V max. Rounded to the nearest milligram per day, the answer is five hundred ninety-five. A K M rounded too early, such as seven point zero milligrams per liter, gives six hundred seven point five milligrams per day.",
  "nl-ic4a": "The patient's Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is fourteen point four milligrams per liter. Two daily phenytoin doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The weight and the volume of distribution are not needed for K M. The top is five hundred minus three hundred, which is two hundred milligrams per day. Three hundred divided by seven point two milligrams per liter is forty-one point six six seven, and five hundred divided by eighteen is twenty-seven point seven seven eight, so the bottom is thirteen point eight eight nine milligrams per day per milligram per liter. Two hundred divided by thirteen point eight eight nine is fourteen point four milligrams per liter. Inverting a ratio, seven point two divided by three hundred, gives a tiny or negative denominator, which signals the slip.",
  "nl-ic4b": "The patient's maximum elimination rate V max, in milligrams per day. The answer is nine hundred milligrams per day. With K M of fourteen point four milligrams per liter from part a, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s. One dose and the steady-state concentration it produced are enough. With the first pair, three hundred milligrams per day and its own seven point two milligrams per liter, three hundred times the sum of fourteen point four and seven point two is six thousand four hundred eighty, and dividing by seven point two gives nine hundred milligrams per day; milligrams per liter cancel. The second pair, five hundred milligrams per day with eighteen milligrams per liter, gives the same nine hundred, as it must for one patient with one V max. Pairing three hundred milligrams with eighteen milligrams per liter would give a different, wrong value. V max must exceed the five hundred milligrams per day dose that reached a steady state.",
  "nl-ic4c": "The recommended daily phenytoin dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred sixty milligrams per day. The patient's own K M, fourteen point four milligrams per liter, and V max, nine hundred milligrams per day, from parts a and b, go into the nonlinear dose-rate line with the target of fifteen milligrams per liter: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. Nine hundred milligrams per day times fifteen milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of fourteen point four and fifteen, which is twenty-nine point four milligrams per liter, is four hundred fifty-nine point two milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. Rounded to the nearest ten milligrams, that is four hundred sixty milligrams per day, a dose that can be given; the unrounded value is also accepted. Fifteen milligrams per liter lies between the seven point two and eighteen measured, so the dose lies between three hundred milligrams per day and five hundred milligrams per day.",
  "nl-ic4d-t": "The elimination half-life at fifteen milligrams per liter, in days. The answer is one point zero five days. For a saturable drug the half-life depends on the concentration, so it is calculated at the stated C p, not from zero point six nine three divided by a fixed k: half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p. The volume is given per kilogram, so first, zero point seven liters per kilogram times sixty-six kilograms gives a V D of forty-six point two liters. The sum of K M and C p is fourteen point four plus fifteen, or twenty-nine point four milligrams per liter. Forty-six point two liters times zero point six nine three divided by nine hundred milligrams per day, times twenty-nine point four milligrams per liter, is one point zero four six days, which rounds to one point zero five. Liters times milligrams per liter leaves milligrams, which cancels the milligrams of V max and leaves days. Leaving V D in liters per kilogram gives zero point zero one six, a result per kilogram, not a time.",
  "nl-p1b": "The recommended daily phenytoin dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred sixty-five milligrams per day. The patient's own K M of five point nine one milligrams per liter and V max of six hundred forty-seven point eight three milligrams per day go into the nonlinear dose-rate line with fifteen milligrams per liter as C s s: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. No weight is needed, because V max is in milligrams per day. Six hundred forty-seven point eight three times fifteen is nine thousand seven hundred seventeen point five, and five point nine one plus fifteen is twenty point nine one milligrams per liter. Dividing gives four hundred sixty-four point seven milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. To the nearest five milligrams that is four hundred sixty-five milligrams per day; the unrounded value is also accepted. Fifteen milligrams per liter lies between five point one and twenty, so the dose lies between the three hundred milligrams per day and five hundred milligrams per day she was given.",
  "nl-p3a": "The Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is nine point four milligrams per liter. Two daily doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The seventy-eight kilogram weight and the seventy percent volume play no part in K M; they are needed only for the half-life. The top is four hundred minus three hundred, which is one hundred milligrams per day. Three hundred divided by ten point four milligrams per liter is twenty-eight point eight four six, and four hundred divided by twenty-two is eighteen point one eight two. With the earlier pair first, the bottom is positive: ten point six six four milligrams per day per milligram per liter. One hundred divided by ten point six six four is nine point three seven seven milligrams per liter, which rounds to nine point four, her sheet's value; the unrounded value is also accepted.",
  "nl-p3b": "The maximum elimination rate V max, in milligrams per day. The answer is five hundred seventy-one milligrams per day. With K M of nine point four milligrams per liter from part a, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s. Her sheet uses the first pair, three hundred milligrams per day with its own ten point four milligrams per liter. Three hundred times the sum of nine point four and ten point four is five thousand nine hundred forty, and dividing by ten point four gives five hundred seventy-one point two milligrams per day; milligrams per liter cancel. The second pair, four hundred milligrams per day with twenty-two milligrams per liter, gives five hundred seventy point nine, which agrees within rounding, as it must for one patient with one V max. Rounded to the nearest milligram per day, V max is five hundred seventy-one, which her sheet carries into the later parts. V max must exceed the four hundred milligrams per day dose that reached a steady state.",
  "nl-p3e": "The daily phenytoin dose R for a target of fifteen milligrams per liter, in milligrams per day. The answer is three hundred fifty milligrams per day. The patient's own K M of nine point four milligrams per liter and V max of five hundred seventy-one milligrams per day, from parts a and b, go into the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. No weight is needed, because V max is in milligrams per day. Five hundred seventy-one milligrams per day times fifteen milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of fifteen and nine point four, which is twenty-four point four milligrams per liter, is three hundred fifty-one point zero two milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. The stem asks for the nearest ten milligrams, so the answer is three hundred fifty milligrams per day, as her sheet gives it. Fifteen milligrams per liter lies between the ten point four and twenty-two measured, so the dose lies between the three hundred milligrams per day and four hundred milligrams per day he took.",
  "nl-p4a": "The Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is nine milligrams per liter. Two daily doses in one patient, each with its steady-state concentration, call for the direct method: K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one divided by C one and R two divided by C two. The sixty-eight kilogram weight and the seventy percent volume are needed only for the half-life, not for K M. The top is four hundred minus three hundred, which is one hundred milligrams per day. Three hundred divided by nine milligrams per liter is thirty-three point three three three, and four hundred divided by eighteen is twenty-two point two two two, so the bottom, earlier pair first, is eleven point one one one milligrams per day per milligram per liter. One hundred divided by eleven point one one one is nine point zero zero zero milligrams per liter, exactly nine, because the bottom is one ninth of one hundred. Here K M equals the nine milligrams per liter measured on three hundred milligrams per day.",
  "nl-p4b": "The maximum elimination rate V max, in milligrams per day. The answer is six hundred milligrams per day. With K M of nine milligrams per liter from part a, the dose-rate line is solved for V max: V max equals R times the sum of K M and C s s, divided by C s s. Her sheet uses the second pair, four hundred milligrams per day with its own eighteen milligrams per liter. Four hundred times the sum of eighteen and nine, divided by eighteen, is six hundred milligrams per day; milligrams per liter cancel, so V max keeps the units of the dose. The first pair, three hundred milligrams per day with nine milligrams per liter, gives five thousand four hundred divided by nine, the same six hundred, as it must for one patient with one V max. Pairing three hundred milligrams per day with eighteen milligrams per liter instead gives four hundred fifty milligrams per day, a mismatched pair. V max must exceed four hundred milligrams per day, the higher dose, because that dose reached a steady state.",
  "nl-p4e": "The daily phenytoin dose R for a target of fifteen milligrams per liter, in milligrams per day. The answer is three hundred seventy-five milligrams per day. The patient's own K M of nine milligrams per liter and V max of six hundred milligrams per day, from parts a and b, go into the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. No weight is needed, because V max is in milligrams per day. Six hundred times fifteen is nine thousand; the bottom, nine plus fifteen, is twenty-four milligrams per liter. Nine thousand divided by twenty-four is exactly three hundred seventy-five milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel; the stem asks for no rounding. Fifteen milligrams per liter lies between the nine and eighteen measured, so the dose lies between the three hundred milligrams per day and four hundred milligrams per day she took. That dose rate is also below the six hundred milligrams per day V max, as every dose rate that reaches a steady state must be.",
  "x3-c-01": "Which statement about a nonlinear drug's elimination rate is true. The answer is: at high concentrations of drug in the plasma relative to K M, the elimination rate is independent of concentration. K M and C p are both concentrations, in milligrams per liter, so they are the pair compared; V max is a rate, in milligrams per day. With C p much greater than K M, V max times C p, divided by the sum of K M and C p, reduces to V max, a constant, so the rate no longer follows the level, which is zero-order behaviour. The tempting options that compare the plasma level with V max fail because a concentration cannot be high or low relative to a rate. The option saying the rate depends on concentration at high levels relative to K M carries the low-concentration behaviour to the high end. She said, So, nutshell, low concentrations of drug in the plasma with respect to K M because remember both of these are gonna be in concentration terms, so we can compare these two, even though they're not the same thing.",
  "x3-c-02": "The comparison that decides whether elimination is first order or zero order. The answer is C p compared with K M. K M, the Michaelis constant, carries units of concentration, as C p does, in milligrams per liter, so the two can be set against each other. Far below K M the rate follows C p, which is first order; far above K M the rate is V max whatever C p is, which is zero order. The tempting option, C p compared with V max, sets a level against a rate: V max is the maximum elimination rate, in milligrams per day, and the per time in its units is what separates V max from K M. The option comparing the daily dose with K M sets a dose rate against a concentration; a dose counts as low only when the level it produces is far below K M. She said, So for those of you that missed the question that we just talked about before the quiz, I know who you are. Right, we just talked about it, right? You know what I'm talking about. K M and concentration.",
  "x3-c-03": "What a larger daily dose of a nonlinear drug changes. Two options are correct: increased half-life and decreased clearance. A higher daily dose gives a higher C p, while V max and K M are constants for a given drug in a given patient, so the dose does not change them. The half-life equals V D times zero point six nine three divided by V max, times the sum of K M and C p, so as C p rises, that sum grows and the half-life lengthens. Cl T equals V max divided by the sum of K M and C p, with C p in the denominator, so a higher level gives a lower clearance; this is why the level rises more than in proportion to the dose. The option increased clearance carries the dose into the clearance, but C p sits in the denominator. The option increased V max treats V max as a property of the dose, and the option decreased V max reads saturation as a loss of capacity. She said, So, concentration, as concentration increases, what's gonna happen to our clearance? It's gonna decrease.",
  "x3-c-04": "The patient's Michaelis constant K M, in milligrams per liter. The answer is nine point four milligrams per liter. At steady state the daily dose rate equals the rate of elimination, and V max is given, so the nonlinear dose-rate line, R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s, has one unknown, K M. Rearranged, K M equals C s s times the difference V max minus R, all divided by R. V max minus R is one hundred seventy-one milligrams per day. Twenty-two milligrams per liter times one hundred seventy-one milligrams per day, divided by four hundred milligrams per day, is nine point four zero five milligrams per liter, because milligrams per day cancel; that rounds to nine point four. The same patient on three hundred milligrams per day at ten point four milligrams per liter gives nine point three nine milligrams per liter, so both doses agree. She said, All right, so here's our equation, and brush up on your algebra skills because we're gonna take this equation and rearrange it and solve for every piece of this.",
  "x3-c-05": "The daily dose rate R for this patient, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred fifty milligrams per day. K M, V max and a target average concentration point to the nonlinear dose-rate line: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. Nine milligrams per kilogram per day times fifteen milligrams per liter, divided by the sum of six and fifteen milligrams per liter, is one hundred thirty-five divided by twenty-one, or six point four three milligrams per kilogram per day; milligrams per liter cancel, so R keeps the per-kilogram units of V max. The seventy kilogram weight turns that rate into a dose: six point four two eight six milligrams per kilogram per day times seventy kilograms is four hundred fifty point zero milligrams per day. Stopping at six point four three milligrams per kilogram per day gives a rate for no particular patient, and four hundred fifty milligrams without per day gives no interval. She said, And if I'm your patient, please don't tell me six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day. Tell me how many, tell me what I need.",
  "x3-c-06": "The daily dose rate R, in milligrams per day. The answer is four hundred five milligrams per day. K M, V max and the target C s s are given, so the nonlinear dose-rate line answers directly: R equals V max times C s s, divided by the sum of K M and C s s. V max is six hundred milligrams per day, already a daily amount, so no weight is needed. C s s of fifteen milligrams per liter goes on top and again underneath, added to K M of seven point two milligrams per liter, for twenty-two point two milligrams per liter. Six hundred times fifteen is nine thousand, and dividing nine thousand by twenty-two point two gives four hundred five point four milligrams per day, because milligrams per liter cancel. To the nearest five that is four hundred five; to the nearest ten it would be four hundred ten. Leaving K M out of the bottom gives V max itself, six hundred, far too high. She said, Don't panic if I ask you to round to the nearest ten, right?"
};
